{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XmPxzB7NFn4Pkgo366pjoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the paper and review context carefully before dispatching the two reviewers.\n\nThe paper proposes \"adaptive b*\" for provenance-weighted pretraining of language models on contaminated corpora. Key claims:\n1. Lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector suffices (vs ModernBERT)\n2. α_eff(b) framework as diagnostic tool (b* overshoots by ~4x)\n3. Adaptive b* as zero-cost reasonable default (~70% of grid-best)\n\nThe review context (worst tier) provides the full experimental design with imagined experiments. Let me check what experiments the paper covers vs what was planned.\n\n\nI'll now send out the two reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work positioning.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41092,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41092},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"2e9fcbb4-6770-4276-a310-eef608aa3b60"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XmPxzB7NFn4Pkgo366pjoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the paper and review context. Dispatching two reviewers in parallel: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41092,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41092},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"d7d7b075-6488-4c66-923b-942e41be8012"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XmPxzB7NFn4Pkgo366pjoT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a blind peer review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Adaptive Provenance Weighting for Contaminated Pretraining Corpora\" proposes adaptive b*, an online method that derives the weighting exponent b in provenance-weighted pretraining (w_i = (1-q_i)^b) from per-batch contamination statistics α̂_eff(b) subject to an ESS constraint, requiring no validation set. Three claimed contributions:\n1. Lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) achieves within 0.003 PPL of ModernBERT 150M (AUC=0.986)\n2. α̂_eff(b) framework as diagnostic tool — b* systematically overshoots grid-optimal by ~4× (median b*≈0.87 vs grid-best b=0.2), but b* trajectory reveals batch-level contamination structure\n3. Adaptive b* as zero-cost reasonable default recovering ~70% of grid-best improvement, degrading under cross-generator and domain-skewed scenarios\n\nAdditionally: loss reweighting vs SIR comparison (claimed contribution #3 in intro).\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n- Data: FineWeb 200M tokens, ρ∈{0.4, 0.6}, synthetic via Qwen3-0.6B (self-gen) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-gen)\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B (primary), Llama-3.2-3B (scale test)\n- Eval: PPL on ~4M token holdout; MMLU/HellaSwag for 3B\n- Grid: b∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5,1.0,1.5,2.0,3.0,5.0}, 3 seeds (42,123,456)\n- Baselines: uniform, detect-and-remove (q>0.5), random reweighting\n- n_min sensitivity: {1,2,3,4,5} with batch_size=4\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: Full grid PPL curve. Grid-best b=0.2 across all seeds. PPL differences are tiny (best 17.625, worst uniform 17.700, range 0.075)\n- Table 2: Aggregate recovery ratio 69.8%\n- Table 3: TF-IDF+LR vs ModernBERT: PPL diff only 0.003\n- Table 4: ρ=0.4 vs 0.6: effect does NOT amplify (Δ drops from 0.04 to 0.03)\n- Table 5: 0.6B vs 3B: b* nearly identical (KS p=0.87), 3B improvement only 0.01 PPL\n- Table 6: n_min sensitivity — only n_min=2 works, narrow sweet spot\n- Table 7: Cross-gen: adaptive LOSES (+0.02 vs uniform)\n- Table 8: Domain-skewed: adaptive LOSES (+0.03 vs uniform)\n- Table 9: Method ranking: grid-best >> adaptive >> detect-remove > uniform ≈ random\n- Table 10: Downstream (MMLU/HellaSwag): no significant differences\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\nThe review context lists these planned experiments. Check which appear in the paper:\n- Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3) for 3 seeds ✓ (Table 1)\n- Post-hoc eval for 9 low-b checkpoints ✓ (Table 1)\n- ρ=0.6 data generation ✓ (implicit)\n- ρ=0.6 uniform baseline ✓ (Table 4)\n- ρ=0.6 adaptive ✓ (Table 4)\n- ρ=0.6 grid b=0.5 ✓ (mentioned in Section 5.3)\n- Llama-3.2-3B setup ✓ (implicit)\n- Llama-3.2-3B uniform ✓ (Table 5, Table 10)\n- Llama-3.2-3B adaptive ✓ (Table 5, Table 10)\n- n_min=1 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- n_min=4 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- n_min=5 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- ModernBERT detector ✓ (Table 3)\n- ModernBERT training ablation ✓ (Table 3)\n- Cross-generator datagen ✓ (Section 5.6)\n- Cross-generator training ✓ (Table 7)\n- Detect-and-remove baseline ✓ (Table 9)\n- Random reweighting baseline ✓ (Table 9)\n- Domain-skewed injection ✓ (Table 8)\n- Downstream eval ✓ (Table 10)\n\nAll 20 planned experiments appear in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the adaptive b* derivation well-motivated? Are the mathematical formulations correct? Is the ESS constraint principled?\n\n2. **Experimental Design** — Are experiments well-controlled? Are comparisons fair? Are there missing baselines or ablations?\n\n3. **Statistical Rigor** — Are effect sizes meaningful? Is seed variance properly accounted for? Are claims supported by the data?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — Be specific. Identify the top 3-5 weaknesses with concrete evidence from the paper.\n\n6. **Strengths** — What does the paper do well?\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** — 3-5 specific questions\n\n8. **Score** — 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n9. **Confidence** — 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. The PPL differences throughout this paper are extremely small (often <0.05 absolute on a base of ~17.7). Consider whether these are meaningful.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41092,"cache_read_input_tokens":22936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41092},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"552df61a-98c4-4a12-913b-4b6add596805"}
