{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DAJpxG313TrwtTzVkQjvdK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012j5xanQG8B2iX191DhgDTC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3510,"cache_read_input_tokens":33021,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3510,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"7c7b88cf-47ec-4da1-aa81-2cb28beca081"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012j5xanQG8B2iX191DhgDTC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\tWeb corpora used for language model pretraining increasingly contain synthetic text of unknown provenance. Detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search on a clean validation set. We propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: a per-batch estimator that derives the optimal exponent from an effective tail-index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of the within-batch score distribution, subject to an effective-sample-size (ESS) constraint. The method requires no validation set, no grid search, and no knowledge of the contamination proportion. Across two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Llama-3.2-3B), two contamination rates (40\\%, 60\\%), cross-generator injection, domain-skewed contamination, and two detector qualities, adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds the grid-search optimum (aggregate ratio $\\geq$88.6\\% at $\\rho$=0.4; surpasses grid-best at $\\rho$=0.6 and on 3B). The $b^*$ trajectory shifts automatically with contamination rate and model scale, providing direct evidence that the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework captures the correct data-driven statistics. Effect amplification from 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement (0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4) to 1.24\\% (3B, $\\rho$=0.4) addresses the small-effect-size concern. Downstream gains on MMLU (+0.7\\%) and HellaSwag (+1.1\\%) confirm transfer beyond perplexity.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t%==============================================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\n35\tLarge language models are pretrained on web-scraped corpora that increasingly contain machine-generated text. Estimates suggest 20--60\\% of web text may be synthetically generated, and the Strong Model Collapse result~\\citep{shumailov2024strong} demonstrates that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws. This motivates principled methods for contamination-robust pretraining.\n36\t\n37\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} introduced the detect-and-reweight paradigm: given per-sample contamination scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ from a binary detector, assign weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ and resample the training data via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR). Their approach is effective but requires the exponent $b$ to be selected empirically per model (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2 $\\leq$360M, $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B) using a clean validation set---a resource that is unavailable in large-scale web pretraining.\n38\t\n39\tWe make two observations. First, the convergence theory of~\\citet{convergence2025} provides a bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ that connects the tail index $\\alpha$ of the data distribution to the rate at which reweighted training converges to the clean distribution. This motivates defining an \\emph{effective tail index} $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a function of the weight exponent. Second, the effective sample size (ESS) of a weighted batch provides a natural constraint against over-aggressive downweighting.\n40\t\n41\tWe propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: for each training batch, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the empirical distribution of $q$-scores, then select $b^*$ to maximize $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ subject to ESS $\\geq n_{\\min}$. This replaces grid search with a closed-form per-batch computation, eliminates the need for a validation set, and adapts automatically to varying contamination levels within and across datasets.\n42\t\n43\tOur contributions are:\n44\t\\begin{enumerate}\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical framework.} We introduce $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ estimation with ESS constraints as a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent, replacing per-model empirical tuning.\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical result.} Adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds grid-search best across model scales, contamination rates, generators, and detector qualities, at zero tuning cost.\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Problem setting.} Unlike Golden Ratio Weighting (which requires known contamination proportion $k$) and unlike validation-based $b$ selection, our method operates with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores.\n48\t\\end{enumerate}\n49\t\n50\t%==============================================================================\n51\t\\section{Related Work}\n52\t\\label{sec:related}\n53\t%==============================================================================\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Synthetic Contamination.}\n56\t\\citet{shumailov2024strong} prove that recursive training on model-generated data causes ``strong model collapse,'' where the tails of the data distribution are progressively lost. This motivates contamination mitigation even when synthetic fractions are small.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}\n59\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} train a binary detector (ModernBERT, AUC=0.986) and weight samples as $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ via SIR resampling. The exponent $b$ is selected per model on a validation set ($b=10$ for $\\leq$360M models, $b=1$ for 1.7B). Our work adopts the same weighting formula but replaces the empirical $b$ with a principled adaptive $b^*$ and implements direct loss reweighting rather than SIR.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Data Mixing and Reweighting.}\n62\tDoReMi~\\citep{xie2023doremi} and DSIR~\\citep{xie2023dsir} optimize domain-level mixing weights; SoftDedup~\\citep{abbas2024softdedup} reweights by duplication degree. These operate at coarser granularity than our instance-level approach. Dynamic loss-based reweighting~\\citep{mindermann2024prioritized} uses per-sample loss rather than detector scores, offering a complementary signal.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}\n65\t\\citet{goldenratio2025} derive a theoretically optimal weight $w^*=1/\\varphi$ under the assumption that the real/synthetic mixing proportion $k$ is known. Our setting assumes $k$ is unknown.\n66\t\n67\t%==============================================================================\n68\t\\section{Method}\n69\t\\label{sec:method}\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n73\t\n74\tLet $\\mathcal{D} = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_N\\}$ be a pretraining corpus containing a mixture of human-written and machine-generated text at an unknown contamination proportion $\\rho$. A pretrained binary detector assigns scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ where higher values indicate higher probability of being synthetic. We train a language model by minimizing the weighted loss:\n75\t\\begin{equation}\n76\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{B}|} w_i \\, \\ell(x_i; \\theta), \\quad w_i = (1 - q_i)^b,\n77\t\\end{equation}\n78\twhere $\\mathcal{B}$ is a training batch and $\\ell$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss. The exponent $b \\geq 0$ controls the downweighting aggressiveness: $b=0$ recovers uniform training, while $b \\to \\infty$ approaches hard filtering.\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Effective Tail Index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$}\n81\t\n82\tMotivated by the convergence bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ of~\\citet{convergence2025}, we define an effective tail index that captures how well a given $b$ suppresses contaminated gradient contributions. For a batch $\\mathcal{B}$ with empirical $q$-score distribution, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the Pareto tail behavior of the weight distribution $\\{w_i = (1-q_i)^b\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$. Intuitively, higher $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ means faster convergence to the clean distribution.\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ with ESS Constraint}\n85\t\n86\tWe select $b^*$ per batch by solving:\n87\t\\begin{equation}\n88\t    b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\; \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b) \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min},\n89\t\\end{equation}\n90\twhere the effective sample size is:\n91\t\\begin{equation}\n92\t    \\mathrm{ESS}(b) = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i\\right)^2}{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i^2}.\n93\t\\end{equation}\n94\t\n95\tThe ESS constraint prevents pathological over-downweighting: without it, the optimizer would choose arbitrarily large $b$, reducing the batch to a single effective sample and causing gradient variance explosion.\n96\t\n97\tWe set $n_{\\min} = 2$ throughout. This choice has a clear interpretation (``at least 2 effective samples per batch'') and is robust: Section~\\ref{sec:nmin_ablation} shows that $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1, 2\\}$ both improve over uniform, $n_{\\min} = 3$ is seed-sensitive, and $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ (i.e., $\\geq$ batch size) degenerates to uniform training.\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n100\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ Reweighting}\n101\t\\label{alg:adaptive}\n102\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n103\t\\REQUIRE Batch $\\mathcal{B} = \\{(x_i, q_i)\\}$, ESS lower bound $n_{\\min}$\n104\t\\STATE Compute $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from $\\{q_i\\}_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}}$ via Pareto tail estimation\n105\t\\STATE Find $b^* = \\arg\\max_{b \\geq 0} \\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ subject to $\\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min}$\n106\t\\STATE Compute weights $w_i = (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$\n107\t\\STATE Return weighted loss $\\mathcal{L} = \\frac{\\sum_i w_i \\, \\ell(x_i;\\theta)}{\\sum_i w_i}$\n108\t\\end{algorithmic}\n109\t\\end{algorithm}\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Loss Reweighting vs.\\ SIR Resampling}\n112\t\n113\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} implement reweighting via SIR: samples are drawn with probability proportional to $w_i$, then trained with uniform loss. We instead apply weights directly to the gradient:\n114\t$\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{\\sum_j w_j} \\sum_i w_i \\nabla_\\theta \\ell(x_i; \\theta)$.\n115\tThis preserves continuous, fine-grained control at the gradient level, avoids the discretization inherent in resampling, and enables per-batch $b^*$ without data preprocessing.\n116\t\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n119\t\\label{sec:setup}\n120\t%==============================================================================\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n123\tWe construct contaminated pretraining corpora from FineWeb subsets (200M tokens). At contamination rate $\\rho \\in \\{0.4, 0.6\\}$, we replace $\\rho$ fraction of the data with synthetic text generated by Qwen3-0.6B (same-generator) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator). A domain-skewed variant injects 70\\% code, 20\\% technical/science, 10\\% general among the synthetic portion.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Detectors.}\n126\tOur primary detector is TF-IDF + Logistic Regression (AUC=0.939, calibrated ECE=0.048). We also evaluate ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986, ECE=0.022) as a high-quality alternative.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n129\tWe train Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B from pretrained checkpoints. Effective batch size is 16 for both (micro-batch 4 $\\times$ gradient accumulation 4), with gradient checkpointing for 3B. Training runs 6,103 steps on 200M tokens.\n130\t\n131\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n132\t\\textbf{Uniform}: standard training ($b=0$). \\textbf{Grid-best}: best $b$ from grid search over $\\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ with 3 seeds. \\textbf{Detect-and-remove}: hard filtering at $q > 0.5$. \\textbf{Random reweight}: i.i.d.\\ uniform $[0,1]$ weights (no detector signal).\n133\t\n134\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n135\tPrimary: holdout perplexity (PPL) on 4.08M tokens. Secondary: MMLU (4-shot), HellaSwag (0-shot), Self-BLEU (4-gram). All experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456) where applicable.\n136\t\n137\t%==============================================================================\n138\t\\section{Results}\n139\t\\label{sec:results}\n140\t%==============================================================================\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Grid Search Landscape and the Optimal Plateau}\n143\t\\label{sec:grid}\n144\t\n145\tTable~\\ref{tab:grid} presents the extended grid search over $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\\}$ at $\\rho=0.4$ on Qwen3-0.6B across 3 seeds. The optimal region is not a single point but a broad plateau spanning $b \\in [0.3, 0.5]$: the cross-seed mean at $b=0.3$ (17.649) and $b=0.5$ (17.646) differ by only 0.003 PPL, with a paired $t$-test $p=0.42$ (n.s., $n=3$). This plateau has an asymmetric shape---PPL decreases slowly for $b < 0.3$ (from 17.680 at $b=0.1$) but rises sharply for $b > 0.5$---implying that overshooting $b$ is less costly than undershooting.\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{table}[t]\n148\t\\centering\n149\t\\caption{Extended grid search: eval PPL ($\\downarrow$) at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens.}\n150\t\\label{tab:grid}\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc c}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\t$b$ & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 & Mean $\\pm$ Std \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t0 (Uniform) & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 & 17.693 $\\pm$ 0.006 \\\\\n157\t0.1 & 17.681 & 17.674 & 17.684 & 17.680 $\\pm$ 0.005 \\\\\n158\t0.2 & 17.668 & 17.656 & 17.670 & 17.665 $\\pm$ 0.008 \\\\\n159\t0.3 & 17.654 & 17.641 & 17.653 & 17.649 $\\pm$ 0.007 \\\\\n160\t\\textbf{0.5} & \\textbf{17.651} & \\textbf{17.638} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{17.646 $\\pm$ 0.007} \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n164\t\n165\t{\\footnotesize Paired $t$-test $b$=0.3 vs $b$=0.5: $p$=0.42 (n.s.). Grid-best is $b$=0.5 but the difference from $b$=0.3 is within seed variance.}\n166\t\\end{table}\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{Adaptive $b^*$ Matches Grid-Best}\n169\t\\label{sec:adaptive_vs_grid}\n170\t\n171\tTable~\\ref{tab:adaptive_main} compares adaptive $b^*$ against the grid-best ($b=0.5$) under the same $\\rho=0.4$ setting. Adaptive achieves an aggregate ratio of 88.6\\% of the grid-best improvement (measured as PPL reduction from uniform), with all three seeds improving over uniform. Critically, adaptive requires a single training run vs.\\ $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs for the full grid search.\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ vs.\\ grid-best ($b$=0.5) at $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B.}\n176\t\\label{tab:adaptive_main}\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\tMethod & seed42 & seed123 & seed456 \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 \\\\\n183\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) PPL & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 \\\\\n184\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.643 & 17.660 \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tRatio (\\% of grid-best gain) & 102.6\\% & 90.4\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n187\t\\bottomrule\n188\t\\end{tabular}\n189\t\\vspace{0.3em}\n190\t\n191\t{\\footnotesize Aggregate ratio: 88.6\\%. All 3 seeds: adaptive $>$ uniform. Adaptive uses 1 run; grid search uses $7 \\times 3 = 21$ runs.}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Contamination Rate Scaling: Effect Amplification}\n195\t\\label{sec:rho_scaling}\n196\t\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:rho_scaling} compares $\\rho=0.4$ and $\\rho=0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). At 60\\% contamination, adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.19 (1.06\\% relative), a 4.75$\\times$ amplification over the 0.04 PPL improvement at 40\\%. Importantly, the fixed grid-best $b=0.5$ (tuned at $\\rho$=0.4) transfers poorly: it captures only 47\\% of the adaptive improvement at $\\rho$=0.6, demonstrating that a fixed $b$ does not generalize across contamination levels.\n198\t\n199\tThe $b^*$ distribution shifts rightward from median 0.87 ($\\rho$=0.4) to 1.42 ($\\rho$=0.6), confirming that the adaptive framework automatically increases downweighting strength as contamination increases. This $b^*$-tracks-optimum behavior provides direct evidence that $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n200\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"bcb4a52d-946d-4682-af50-24817e8c4371","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:21:27.667Z"}
