{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SHHopFXbbLSygonJM6cZQi","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\n201\t\\begin{table}[t]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\caption{Contamination rate scaling ($\\rho$=0.4 vs $\\rho$=0.6), Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n204\t\\label{tab:rho_scaling}\n205\t\\small\n206\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n207\t\\toprule\n208\t& $\\rho=0.4$ & $\\rho=0.6$ \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.920 \\\\\n211\tGrid $b$=0.5 PPL & 17.651 & 17.830 \\\\\n212\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 17.730 \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\t$\\Delta$PPL (adaptive vs uniform) & 0.040 & 0.190 \\\\\n215\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.06 \\\\\n216\tEffect amplification & --- & 4.75$\\times$ \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 1.42 \\\\\n219\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 2.08 \\\\\n220\tRatio vs grid $b$=0.5 (\\%) & 102.6 & 111.8 \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Scaling: Qwen3-0.6B vs Llama-3.2-3B}\n226\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n227\t\n228\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} shows results on Llama-3.2-3B at $\\rho=0.4$. The 3B model exhibits 5.39$\\times$ effect amplification over 0.6B: adaptive improves PPL by 0.16 (1.24\\% relative) vs.\\ 0.04 (0.23\\%) on 0.6B. The $b^*$ distribution is nearly identical across model scales (median 0.88 vs 0.87; mean 1.41 vs 1.40), confirming that $b^*$ is a \\emph{data property}, not a model property---adaptive requires no per-model tuning.\n229\t\n230\t\\begin{table}[t]\n231\t\\centering\n232\t\\caption{Cross-model results at $\\rho$=0.4, seed42, 200M tokens.}\n233\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n234\t\\small\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t& Qwen3-0.6B & Llama-3.2-3B \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 12.850 \\\\\n240\tAdaptive $b^*$ PPL & 17.650 & 12.690 \\\\\n241\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.160 \\\\\n242\tRelative improvement (\\%) & 0.23 & 1.24 \\\\\n243\tEffect amplification & --- & 5.39$\\times$ \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.88 \\\\\n246\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 1.41 \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{$b^*$ Distribution Analysis}\n252\t\\label{sec:bstar_dist}\n253\t\n254\tTable~\\ref{tab:bstar_dist} summarizes the $b^*$ distribution across experimental conditions. Two patterns emerge: (1) $b^*$ shifts rightward with contamination rate ($\\rho$=0.6 median 1.42 vs $\\rho$=0.4 median 0.87), reflecting the need for stronger downweighting at higher contamination; (2) $b^*$ is invariant to model scale (0.6B vs 3B) and adjusts to detector quality (ModernBERT median 0.45 vs TF-IDF median 0.87, as the more precise detector requires less aggressive weighting).\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[t]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\caption{$b^*$ distribution summary across settings.}\n259\t\\label{tab:bstar_dist}\n260\t\\small\n261\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n262\t\\toprule\n263\tSetting & Median & Mean & P25 & P75 \\\\\n264\t\\midrule\n265\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.87 & 1.40 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n266\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.6, TF-IDF & 1.42 & 2.08 & 0.82 & 2.55 \\\\\n267\tLlama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, TF-IDF & 0.88 & 1.41 & 0.54 & 1.53 \\\\\n268\tQwen-0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4, ModernBERT & 0.45 & 0.82 & --- & --- \\\\\n269\tCross-gen (Llama$\\to$Qwen), $\\rho$=0.4 & 0.83 & 1.32 & --- & --- \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\end{table}\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{$n_{\\min}$ Sensitivity Ablation}\n275\t\\label{sec:nmin_ablation}\n276\t\n277\tTable~\\ref{tab:nmin} presents the ESS constraint sensitivity ($\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42). The results reveal a clear phase structure: $n_{\\min}=1$ is over-aggressive ($b^*$ median 1.73, 12.8\\% of batches with $b^*>5$) yet still improves over uniform (PPL 17.668); $n_{\\min}=2$ is optimal (PPL 17.650); $n_{\\min} \\geq 4$ ($\\geq$ batch size) forces $b^*=0$ and degenerates exactly to uniform (PPL 17.690). This confirms that $n_{\\min}$ is not a sensitive hyperparameter: $n_{\\min} \\in \\{1,2\\}$ both work, while values $\\geq$ batch size are degenerate by construction.\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\caption{ESS constraint ($n_{\\min}$) sensitivity, $\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42.}\n282\t\\label{tab:nmin}\n283\t\\small\n284\t\\begin{tabular}{l cccc}\n285\t\\toprule\n286\t$n_{\\min}$ & Eval PPL & $b^*$ median & $b^*$ mean & Note \\\\\n287\t\\midrule\n288\t1 & 17.668 & 1.73 & 2.95 & Over-aggressive \\\\\n289\t\\textbf{2} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{1.40} & \\textbf{Optimal} \\\\\n290\t4 (=batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & $\\equiv$ Uniform \\\\\n291\t5 ($>$batch) & 17.690 & 0.00 & 0.00 & Degenerate \\\\\n292\t\\bottomrule\n293\t\\end{tabular}\n294\t\\end{table}\n295\t\n296\t\\subsection{Detector Quality Ablation}\n297\t\\label{sec:detector}\n298\t\n299\tTable~\\ref{tab:detector} compares the lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) against ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986). ModernBERT improves adaptive PPL by an additional 0.012 (from 17.650 to 17.638), a 30\\% increase in reweighting value. However, the TF-IDF detector already captures 77\\% of the ModernBERT-level improvement at orders-of-magnitude lower cost (CPU-only vs.\\ GPU fine-tuning of a 150M transformer). This supports the practical claim that a lightweight detector suffices.\n300\t\n301\tThe $b^*$ distribution adapts to detector quality: ModernBERT's more bimodal $q$-scores (mid-range ratio 0.28 vs.\\ 0.46 for TF-IDF) lead to lower $b^*$ (median 0.45 vs.\\ 0.87), since less aggressive weighting is needed when scores are already well-separated.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[t]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Detector quality ablation ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n306\t\\label{tab:detector}\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n309\t\\toprule\n310\t& TF-IDF+LR & ModernBERT \\\\\n311\t\\midrule\n312\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.986 \\\\\n313\tCalibrated ECE & 0.048 & 0.022 \\\\\n314\tMid-range ratio ($q \\in [0.2, 0.8]$) & 0.463 & 0.280 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 \\\\\n317\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.638 \\\\\n318\t$\\Delta$PPL (improvement) & 0.040 & 0.052 \\\\\n319\tGrid-best PPL & 17.646 & 17.634 \\\\\n320\tAdaptive / Grid ratio (\\%) & 88.6 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.45 \\\\\n323\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 0.82 \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n329\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n330\t\n331\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_gen} evaluates robustness when synthetic data is generated by Llama-3.2-1B (a different model family) rather than Qwen3-0.6B (the training model). The TF-IDF detector achieves higher AUC (0.952) on cross-generator data, as cross-family generation artifacts are more distinctive. Adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.05 (0.28\\% relative), comparable to the same-generator result (0.04 PPL, 0.23\\%), confirming generator-agnostic operation.\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[t]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Cross-generator results ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n336\t\\label{tab:cross_gen}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t& Same-gen (Qwen) & Cross-gen (Llama) \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.952 \\\\\n343\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.720 \\\\\n344\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.670 \\\\\n345\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.050 \\\\\n346\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.83 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Contamination}\n352\t\\label{sec:domain_skew}\n353\t\n354\tReal web corpora exhibit non-uniform contamination across domains. Table~\\ref{tab:domain_skew} tests a domain-skewed injection (70\\% code, 20\\% technical, 10\\% general among synthetic data). Adaptive $b^*$ improves by 0.05 PPL (0.28\\% relative), matching uniform-injection performance. Per-batch $b^*$ varies by domain: code-heavy batches receive median $b^*=1.35$ (stronger downweighting) while general-domain batches receive 0.42, demonstrating the per-batch adaptivity that fixed-$b$ methods cannot achieve.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\caption{Domain-skewed injection ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B).}\n359\t\\label{tab:domain_skew}\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{l c}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\tMetric & Value \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tContamination distribution & 70\\% code / 20\\% tech / 10\\% general \\\\\n366\tUniform PPL & 17.730 \\\\\n367\tAdaptive PPL & 17.680 \\\\\n368\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.050 \\\\\n369\t$b^*$ median (code batches) & 1.35 \\\\\n370\t$b^*$ median (general batches) & 0.42 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n376\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n377\t\n378\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares all methods at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42. Adaptive $b^*$ achieves the best PPL (17.650). Detect-and-remove ($q>0.5$) achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Random reweighting performs equivalently to uniform (PPL 17.695 vs 17.690), confirming that the improvement derives from detector-informed weighting, not from weight diversity or regularization effects.\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{table}[t]\n381\t\\centering\n382\t\\caption{Baseline comparison ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n383\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n384\t\\small\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tMethod & Eval PPL & $\\Delta$ vs Uniform & Data Used \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tUniform ($b$=0) & 17.690 & --- & 100\\% \\\\\n390\tRandom reweight & 17.695 & $-$0.005 & 100\\% \\\\\n391\tDetect-and-remove ($q$>0.5) & 17.668 & +0.022 & 53.7\\% \\\\\n392\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) & 17.651 & +0.039 & 100\\% \\\\\n393\t\\textbf{Adaptive $b^*$} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{+0.040} & \\textbf{100\\%} \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Downstream Task Evaluation}\n399\t\\label{sec:downstream}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"cc2b62c9-c143-416d-8702-af781272c4b2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:21:32.590Z"}
