{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JEPTQTK9SmrZbLUioxiBS4","type":"tool_result","content":"399\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n400\t\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} evaluates Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints on downstream tasks. Adaptive $b^*$ improves MMLU by +0.7\\% absolute (31.8$\\to$32.5, knowledge recall) and HellaSwag by +1.1\\% (52.1$\\to$53.2, commonsense reasoning). Self-BLEU decreases from 0.42 to 0.38, indicating reduced repetitive generation---a direct symptom of model collapse that adaptive reweighting mitigates.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[t]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, seed42).}\n406\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t& Uniform & Adaptive $b^*$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tMMLU (4-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 31.8 & 32.5 & +0.7 \\\\\n413\tHellaSwag (0-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 52.1 & 53.2 & +1.1 \\\\\n414\tSelf-BLEU (4-gram, $\\downarrow$) & 0.42 & 0.38 & $-$0.04 \\\\\n415\t\\bottomrule\n416\t\\end{tabular}\n417\t\\end{table}\n418\t\n419\t%==============================================================================\n420\t\\section{Analysis}\n421\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n422\t%==============================================================================\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{$b^*$ tracks the optimum.}\n425\tThe most striking finding is that the $b^*$ distribution shifts systematically with experimental conditions while requiring no manual adjustment. When contamination increases from 40\\% to 60\\%, median $b^*$ rises from 0.87 to 1.42---matching the shift in the grid-optimal region. When a more precise detector is used (ModernBERT), $b^*$ decreases to median 0.45 because less aggressive weighting suffices for better-separated scores. When contamination is domain-skewed, per-batch $b^*$ differentiates code-heavy batches (1.35) from general batches (0.42). These adaptations happen automatically through the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, validating that it captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Effect amplification addresses the small-effect concern.}\n428\tAt 0.6B/$\\rho$=0.4, the 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement might appear small. However, this scales substantially: 1.06\\% at $\\rho$=0.6 (4.75$\\times$) and 1.24\\% at 3B/$\\rho$=0.4 (5.39$\\times$). Downstream gains of +0.7\\% MMLU and +1.1\\% HellaSwag on 3B further demonstrate that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining produces measurable improvements in knowledge recall and commonsense reasoning. The value proposition is principled, zero-cost, and effective: adaptive $b^*$ is strictly better than uniform at no additional tuning cost.\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Lightweight detector sufficiency.}\n431\tTF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) achieves 77\\% of the reweighting value of ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986), at negligible computational cost. This is practically significant: practitioners need not train a large neural detector to benefit from provenance-aware reweighting.\n432\t\n433\t\\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs hard filtering.}\n434\tDetect-and-remove achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Under an imperfect detector (AUC $<$ 1.0), continuous weights are more tolerant of detection errors than binary decisions: a sample with $q=0.48$ (retained) and $q=0.52$ (removed) receive vastly different treatment under hard filtering but similar weights under soft reweighting.\n435\t\n436\t%==============================================================================\n437\t\\section{Conclusion}\n438\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n439\t%==============================================================================\n440\t\n441\tWe presented adaptive $b^*$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware language model pretraining. By deriving $b^*$ from the effective tail index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of per-batch score distributions subject to an ESS constraint, our approach eliminates the need for validation-based grid search while matching or exceeding grid-optimal performance. The $b^*$ trajectory adapts automatically to contamination rate, model scale, detector quality, generator identity, and domain composition, confirming that the framework captures meaningful data statistics rather than requiring per-setting tuning.\n442\t\n443\tEffect amplification at higher contamination rates (4.75$\\times$ at $\\rho$=0.6) and larger models (5.39$\\times$ at 3B) suggests that adaptive $b^*$ becomes increasingly valuable precisely where contamination matters most. The method is principled (grounded in convergence-motivated $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ estimation), zero-cost (no validation runs), and effective (consistent improvements across all tested settings).\n444\t\n445\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n446\tOur experiments use controlled synthetic injection rather than organically contaminated web data; results on truly ``in the wild'' contamination remain to be validated. The method inherits the quality of the upstream detector---if the detector fails to distinguish real from synthetic text (e.g., for high-quality human-like generation), reweighting cannot help. We evaluate at up to 3B parameters and 200M tokens; scaling behavior at 7B+ and trillion-token scale is an open question.\n447\t\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t% References\n450\t%==============================================================================\n451\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n452\t\n453\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Drayson et~al.(2025)]{drayson2025contamination}\n456\tDrayson, N., et~al.\n457\t\\newblock Detect and remove: Addressing synthetic data contamination in model training.\n458\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, 2025.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024strong}\n461\tShumailov, I., Shumaylov, Z., Zhao, Y., Gal, Y., Papernot, N., and Anderson, R.\n462\t\\newblock AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n463\t\\newblock \\emph{Nature}, 2024.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[{Convergence Theory}(2025)]{convergence2025}\n466\tAnonymous.\n467\t\\newblock Convergence bounds for reweighted distributions under tail-index conditions.\n468\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2602.16065}, 2025.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem[{Golden Ratio}(2025)]{goldenratio2025}\n471\tAnonymous.\n472\t\\newblock Golden ratio weighting for synthetic data mixtures.\n473\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.18049}, 2025.\n474\t\n475\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023a)]{xie2023doremi}\n476\tXie, S.~M., Pham, H., Dong, X., Du, N., Liu, H., Lu, Y., Liang, P., Le, Q.~V., Ma, T., and Yu, A.~W.\n477\t\\newblock DoReMi: Optimizing data mixtures speeds up language model pretraining.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023b)]{xie2023dsir}\n481\tXie, S.~M., Santurkar, S., Ma, T., and Liang, P.\n482\t\\newblock Data selection for language models via importance resampling.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n484\t\n485\t\\bibitem[Abbas et~al.(2024)]{abbas2024softdedup}\n486\tAbbas, A., et~al.\n487\t\\newblock SoftDedup: Soft deduplication for pretraining data.\n488\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem[Mindermann et~al.(2024)]{mindermann2024prioritized}\n491\tMindermann, S., et~al.\n492\t\\newblock Prioritized training on points that are learnable, worth learning, and not yet learned.\n493\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n494\t\n495\t\\end{thebibliography}\n496\t\n497\t\\end{document}\n498\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"740c8f96-952c-4e38-99d2-0c4fa983ace1","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:21:37.575Z"}
