{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013eWYm4hcuwuV2znJyz3Yc4","type":"tool_result","content":"50\tOur investigation yields three contributions, ordered by strength of evidence:\n51\t\n52\t\\begin{enumerate}\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{Lightweight detector sufficiency.} A TF-IDF + logistic regression detector (50K parameters, AUC = 0.939, $<$1 min CPU training) produces training outcomes within 0.003 PPL of a ModernBERT 150M detector (AUC = 0.986, 1.5h GPU training).\n54\t    The marginal return of detector quality on downstream PPL is negligible, suggesting that practitioners can skip expensive neural detectors entirely.\n55\t\n56\t    \\item \\textbf{$\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a diagnostic framework.} We formalize the connection between batch-level contamination statistics and the weighting exponent through $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$.\n57\t    While the derived $b^*$ systematically overshoots the grid-optimal value (median $b^* \\approx 0.87$ versus grid-best $b = 0.2$), the $b^*$ trajectory across batches and contamination levels provides interpretable diagnostics about contamination structure that the original framework lacks.\n58\t\n59\t    \\item \\textbf{Loss reweighting vs.\\ SIR.} We provide a systematic comparison of continuous loss reweighting and discrete SIR under identical weighting formulas, characterizing their gradient variance and convergence behavior differences.\n60\t\\end{enumerate}\n61\t\n62\tAs an additional practical finding, adaptive $b^*$ recovers approximately 70\\% of grid-best improvement at zero tuning cost, serving as a reasonable default in standard settings---though it degrades or becomes harmful under cross-generator contamination (Section~\\ref{sec:cross_gen}) and domain-skewed injection (Section~\\ref{sec:domain_skew}).\n63\t\n64\t% ============================================================\n65\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n66\t\\label{sec:background}\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Synthetic Contamination.}\n69\tTraining on model-generated data can cause progressive degradation of output quality, termed \\emph{model collapse}~\\citep{shumailov2024strong}.\n70\tEven low contamination ratios degrade scaling behavior, motivating methods that mitigate synthetic content during pretraining.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Detect-and-Reweight.}\n73\tDrayson et al.\\ train a detector to produce scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ indicating the probability that sample $i$ is synthetic.\n74\tEach sample receives weight $w_i = (1 - q_i)^b$, where $b > 0$ controls downweighting aggressiveness.\n75\tTheir implementation uses SIR: samples are stochastically resampled proportional to their weights, effectively a discrete selection mechanism.\n76\tThe exponent $b$ is selected via grid search over a validation set, yielding model-specific values ($b = 10$ for $\\leq$360M models, $b = 1$ for 1.7B).\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Golden Ratio Weighting.}\n79\tConcurrent work derives a theoretically optimal weight $w^* = 1/\\varphi$ but assumes the real-to-synthetic ratio $k$ is known, which is unrealistic for web corpora.\n80\t\n81\t\\paragraph{Domain-Level Reweighting.}\n82\tDoReMi and DSIR optimize domain-level mixture weights, operating at a coarser granularity than our instance-level approach.\n83\tSoftDedup applies soft reweighting based on duplication, which is orthogonal to provenance-based weighting.\n84\tDynamic loss-based reweighting adjusts weights based on training loss rather than detector scores, providing a complementary signal.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Convergence Theory.}\n87\tThe bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p, \\alpha\\}}$ from recent work motivates the definition of $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ and the derivation of $b^*$, though we do not claim this theoretical bridge as a contribution.\n88\t\n89\t% ============================================================\n90\t\\section{Method}\n91\t\\label{sec:method}\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n94\t\n95\tWe consider pretraining on a corpus $\\mathcal{D} = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_N\\}$ where an unknown fraction $\\rho$ of samples are machine-generated.\n96\tA pretrained detector provides scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ estimating $P(\\text{synthetic} \\mid x_i)$.\n97\tOur goal: assign weights $w_i = (1 - q_i)^b$ that mitigate contamination effects, with $b$ determined automatically.\n98\t\n99\t\\subsection{Effective Contamination Fraction $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$}\n100\t\n101\tFor a batch $\\mathcal{B}$ of size $n$, we define the effective contamination fraction under weighting exponent $b$:\n102\t\\begin{equation}\n103\t    \\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b) = 1 - \\frac{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} (1 - q_i)^b}{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} 1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} (1 - q_i)^b.\n104\t\\end{equation}\n105\tThis quantity measures how much effective mass is removed by the weighting scheme: when $b = 0$, $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}} = 0$ (no downweighting); as $b \\to \\infty$, $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}} \\to 1 - (1-\\rho_{\\mathcal{B}})$ where $\\rho_{\\mathcal{B}}$ is the batch contamination fraction for perfectly calibrated $q_i$.\n106\t\n107\t\\subsection{ESS-Constrained $b^*$ Derivation}\n108\t\n109\tWe derive $b^*$ by maximizing the contamination removal subject to maintaining sufficient effective sample size.\n110\tThe ESS for weights $\\{w_i\\}$ in batch $\\mathcal{B}$ is:\n111\t\\begin{equation}\n112\t    \\mathrm{ESS}(\\mathcal{B}, b) = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i\\right)^2}{\\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{B}} w_i^2} = \\frac{\\left(\\sum_{i} (1-q_i)^b\\right)^2}{\\sum_{i} (1-q_i)^{2b}}.\n113\t\\end{equation}\n114\tWe impose $\\mathrm{ESS}(\\mathcal{B}, b) \\geq n_{\\min}$ and solve for the largest $b$ satisfying this constraint:\n115\t\\begin{equation}\n116\t    b^* = \\max \\{ b \\geq 0 : \\mathrm{ESS}(\\mathcal{B}, b) \\geq n_{\\min} \\}.\n117\t\\end{equation}\n118\t\n119\tThe hyperparameter $n_{\\min}$ controls the aggressiveness of downweighting.\n120\tUnlike the continuous exponent $b$ which spans $[0, \\infty)$ and requires multi-point grid search, $n_{\\min}$ takes small integer values ($\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n\\}$) with $n_{\\min} = 2$ performing well across our experiments (Section~\\ref{sec:nmin}).\n121\t\n122\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n123\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ with ESS Constraint}\n124\t\\label{alg:adaptive}\n125\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n126\t\\REQUIRE Batch $\\mathcal{B} = \\{(x_i, q_i)\\}_{i=1}^n$, ESS lower bound $n_{\\min}$, search range $[0, b_{\\max}]$\n127\t\\STATE Compute $\\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ for $b \\in \\{0, 0.1, 0.2, \\ldots, b_{\\max}\\}$ via Eq.~(2)\n128\t\\STATE $b^* \\leftarrow \\max\\{b : \\mathrm{ESS}(b) \\geq n_{\\min}\\}$\n129\t\\STATE $w_i \\leftarrow (1 - q_i)^{b^*}$ for each $i \\in \\mathcal{B}$\n130\t\\STATE $\\bar{w} \\leftarrow \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i w_i$; \\quad $\\tilde{w}_i \\leftarrow w_i / \\bar{w}$ \\hfill (normalize to mean 1)\n131\t\\STATE $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{weighted}} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i \\tilde{w}_i \\cdot \\ell(x_i; \\theta)$\n132\t\\RETURN $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{weighted}}$, $b^*$\n133\t\\end{algorithmic}\n134\t\\end{algorithm}\n135\t\n136\t\\subsection{Loss Reweighting vs.\\ SIR}\n137\t\n138\tDrayson et al.\\ implement provenance weighting via SIR: samples are resampled proportional to $w_i$ before each epoch, creating a modified dataset.\n139\tWe instead apply weights directly to the loss function (Algorithm~\\ref{alg:adaptive}, line 5).\n140\tThe key differences are:\n141\t\\begin{itemize}\n142\t    \\item \\textbf{Granularity}: Loss reweighting applies continuous weights per gradient step; SIR makes discrete include/exclude decisions per epoch.\n143\t    \\item \\textbf{Gradient variance}: Loss reweighting introduces weight-dependent variance in gradient estimates; SIR introduces resampling variance.\n144\t    \\item \\textbf{Adaptivity}: Per-batch $b^*$ naturally integrates with loss reweighting (weights change every batch); SIR would require re-resampling per batch, negating its data-loading efficiency.\n145\t\\end{itemize}\n146\t\n147\t% ============================================================\n148\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n149\t\\label{sec:setup}\n150\t\n151\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n152\tWe use FineWeb subsets totaling 200M tokens.\n153\tSynthetic data is generated by Qwen3-0.6B (self-generation setting) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-generator setting) via continuation of real text prefixes.\n154\tContamination ratios: $\\rho \\in \\{0.4, 0.6\\}$.\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Detectors.}\n157\t(1)~\\textbf{TF-IDF + LR}: TF-IDF features with logistic regression, $\\sim$50K parameters, $<$1 min CPU training, AUC = 0.939.\n158\t(2)~\\textbf{ModernBERT 150M}: Fine-tuned ModernBERT, 150M parameters, 1.5h GPU training, AUC = 0.986.\n159\t\n160\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n161\tQwen3-0.6B (primary) and Llama-3.2-3B (scale validation), trained for 6103 steps on 200M tokens with batch size 4 and gradient checkpointing.\n162\t\n163\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n164\tPerplexity (PPL) on a held-out FineWeb subset ($\\sim$4M tokens).\n165\tDownstream: MMLU (5-shot) and HellaSwag (10-shot) for the 3B model.\n166\tAll experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456) where budget permits.\n167\t\n168\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n169\t\\textit{Uniform}: standard training without reweighting.\n170\t\\textit{Grid-$b$}: fixed $b \\in \\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0\\}$.\n171\t\\textit{Detect-and-remove}: discard samples with $q > 0.5$.\n172\t\\textit{Random reweighting}: weights drawn uniformly from $[0, 2]$ (mean 1).\n173\t\n174\t% ============================================================\n175\t\\section{Results}\n176\t\\label{sec:results}\n177\t\n178\t\\subsection{Full Grid Curve Reveals Low-$b$ Optimum}\n179\t\\label{sec:grid_curve}\n180\t\n181\tTable~\\ref{tab:full_grid} presents the complete PPL landscape across 9 fixed-$b$ values, adaptive $b^*$, and the uniform baseline ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens).\n182\t\n183\t\\begin{table}[t]\n184\t\\centering\n185\t\\caption{Eval PPL across weighting exponents ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, 200M tokens). Lower is better. Bold: best per seed.}\n186\t\\label{tab:full_grid}\n187\t\\small\n188\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n189\t\\toprule\n190\t$b$ & seed 42 & seed 123 & seed 456 \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\t0.1     & 17.645 & 17.631 & 17.642 \\\\\n193\t\\textbf{0.2}     & \\textbf{17.637} & \\textbf{17.625} & \\textbf{17.636} \\\\\n194\t0.3     & 17.641 & 17.629 & 17.640 \\\\\n195\t0.5     & 17.651 & 17.638 & 17.650 \\\\\n196\t1.0     & 17.692 & 17.674 & 17.680 \\\\\n197\t1.5     & 17.718 & 17.701 & 17.710 \\\\\n198\t2.0     & 17.741 & 17.725 & $\\sim$17.73 \\\\\n199\t3.0     & 17.774 & 17.760 & $\\sim$17.77 \\\\\n200\t5.0     & 17.818 & 17.804 & $\\sim$17.82 \\\\\n201\t\\midrule\n202\tUniform & 17.690 & 17.690 & 17.700 \\\\\n203\tAdaptive $b^*$ & 17.650 & 17.643 & 17.660 \\\\\n204\t\\bottomrule\n205\t\\end{tabular}\n206\t\\end{table}\n207\t\n208\tThe grid-best exponent is $b = 0.2$ across all three seeds, with PPL improvements of 0.053, 0.065, and 0.064 over uniform (seed 42, 123, 456 respectively).\n209\tThe PPL curve is monotonically increasing for $b > 0.2$, confirming that aggressive downweighting hurts: at $b = 0.2$, contaminated samples retain 63\\% weight ($w = (1-0.9)^{0.2} = 0.63$), preserving useful signal while mildly correcting distributional shift.\n210\t\n211\tAdaptive $b^*$ (median $\\approx 0.87$) overshoots the grid optimum by approximately $4\\times$.\n212\tThis systematic overshoot reduces the aggregate recovery ratio from the previously reported 88.6\\% (when $b = 0.5$ was believed to be grid-best) to 69.8\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:ratio}).\n213\t\n214\t\\begin{table}[t]\n215\t\\centering\n216\t\\caption{Adaptive $b^*$ recovery ratio relative to grid-best ($b = 0.2$). The gap between adaptive and grid-best (0.013--0.024) exceeds cross-seed variance ($\\sim$0.007).}\n217\t\\label{tab:ratio}\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\tSeed & Uniform & Grid-best ($b\\!=\\!0.2$) & Adaptive & Ratio \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\t42  & 17.690 & 17.637 & 17.650 & 75.5\\% \\\\\n224\t123 & 17.690 & 17.625 & 17.643 & 72.3\\% \\\\\n225\t456 & 17.700 & 17.636 & 17.660 & 62.5\\% \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\t\\textit{Aggregate} & --- & \\textit{0.182} & \\textit{0.127} & \\textit{69.8\\%} \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\subsection{Lightweight Detector Sufficiency}\n233\t\\label{sec:detector}\n234\t\n235\tTable~\\ref{tab:detector} compares the TF-IDF + LR and ModernBERT detectors.\n236\tDespite a large AUC gap (0.939 vs.\\ 0.986), the PPL difference is negligible: 0.003 absolute, $<$0.02\\% relative.\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[t]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\caption{Detector quality vs.\\ training PPL ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, adaptive $b^*$, seed 42). AUC improves by 5\\% relative, but PPL gain is negligible.}\n241\t\\label{tab:detector}\n242\t\\small\n243\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n244\t\\toprule\n245\tDetector & Params & AUC & ECE (cal.) & Adaptive PPL & Cost \\\\\n246\t\\midrule\n247\tTF-IDF + LR   & $\\sim$50K & 0.939 & 0.048 & 17.650 & $<$1 min CPU \\\\\n248\tModernBERT 150M & 150M     & 0.986 & 0.022 & 17.647 & 1.5h GPU \\\\\n249\t\\midrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"ac9e80a2-babd-49a6-86d0-9f93d74776d3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:21:58.262Z"}
