{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kt5GueyMYXeer47aEwwvvA","type":"tool_result","content":"250\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{$\\Delta$ (AUC $\\uparrow$ 0.047)} & & & $-$0.003 & \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\tThe monotonicity of $w = (1-q)^b$ guarantees that ranking correctness matters more than calibration accuracy.\n256\tTF-IDF + LR, despite higher calibration error (ECE 0.048 vs.\\ 0.022) and a more diffuse $q$-score distribution (mid-range ratio 0.463 vs.\\ 0.28), preserves sufficient ranking quality for effective reweighting.\n257\tThe practical implication: a detector trainable in under one minute on a CPU suffices for provenance weighting.\n258\t\n259\t\\subsection{Higher Contamination ($\\rho = 0.6$)}\n260\t\\label{sec:rho06}\n261\t\n262\tTable~\\ref{tab:rho06} compares reweighting effectiveness at 40\\% and 60\\% contamination.\n263\tAt $\\rho = 0.6$, uniform PPL rises from 17.69 to 17.85 (+0.9\\% relative), confirming that higher contamination degrades training quality.\n264\tHowever, adaptive improvement does \\emph{not} scale with contamination: $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}$ decreases from 0.04 to 0.03.\n265\t\n266\t\\begin{table}[t]\n267\t\\centering\n268\t\\caption{Contamination level vs.\\ adaptive effectiveness (Qwen3-0.6B, seed 42). Higher contamination does not amplify the benefit of adaptive reweighting.}\n269\t\\label{tab:rho06}\n270\t\\small\n271\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n272\t\\toprule\n273\t$\\rho$ & Uniform & Adaptive & $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{abs}}$ & $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{rel}}$ (\\%) & $b^*$ median \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\t0.4 & 17.690 & 17.650 & 0.040 & 0.23 & 0.87 \\\\\n276\t0.6 & 17.850 & 17.820 & 0.030 & 0.17 & 1.08 \\\\\n277\t\\bottomrule\n278\t\\end{tabular}\n279\t\\end{table}\n280\t\n281\tThe adaptive mechanism responds directionally: $b^*$ median increases from 0.87 to 1.08 as contamination rises, reflecting higher batch-level $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}$.\n282\tHowever, this response exacerbates overshoot---the true optimal $b$ likely remains near 0.2, so the increased $b^*$ at $\\rho = 0.6$ moves further from the optimum.\n283\tAt $\\rho = 0.6$, grid $b = 0.5$ achieves PPL = 17.80, yielding $\\Delta = 0.05$ versus adaptive's 0.03, for a ratio of only 60\\%.\n284\t\n285\tThis result has diagnostic value: the $b^*$ shift from 0.87 to 1.08 correctly signals increased contamination, even though the derived value is not numerically optimal.\n286\tThe $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ framework thus serves as a contamination \\emph{thermometer}, not an optimizer.\n287\t\n288\t\\subsection{Model Scale (Llama-3.2-3B)}\n289\t\\label{sec:3b}\n290\t\n291\tWe evaluate on Llama-3.2-3B to test whether the method adapts to model scale (Table~\\ref{tab:3b}).\n292\t\n293\t\\begin{table}[t]\n294\t\\centering\n295\t\\caption{$b^*$ distribution comparison across model scales ($\\rho = 0.4$, seed 42). The $b^*$ distribution is nearly identical because it depends only on $q$-score statistics, not model architecture. KS test $p = 0.87$.}\n296\t\\label{tab:3b}\n297\t\\small\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tModel & $b^*$ med. & $b^*$ mean & $b^*$ P25 & $b^*$ P75 & $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{rel}}$ (\\%) \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\tQwen3-0.6B   & 0.87 & 1.40 & 0.54 & 1.51 & 0.23 \\\\\n303\tLlama-3.2-3B & 0.92 & 1.38 & 0.55 & 1.48 & 0.06 \\\\\n304\t\\bottomrule\n305\t\\end{tabular}\n306\t\\end{table}\n307\t\n308\tThe $b^*$ distributions are statistically indistinguishable (KS test $p = 0.87$) because $b^*$ is derived solely from $q$-score statistics, which depend on the data and detector, not the training model.\n309\tThe PPL improvement for 3B is minimal: 0.01 absolute (16.19 vs.\\ 16.20), 0.06\\% relative---within noise.\n310\t\n311\tThis reveals a fundamental property: adaptive $b^*$ provides a \\emph{data-driven} default that is consistent across models.\n312\tThis consistency is both a strength (predictable behavior) and a limitation (no model-specific adaptation).\n313\tDrayson et al.'s observation that different model scales need different $b$ values (1 vs.\\ 10) suggests that optimal $b$ is model-dependent, a dimension our $q$-score-only derivation cannot capture.\n314\t\n315\t\\subsection{ESS Constraint Sensitivity ($n_{\\min}$)}\n316\t\\label{sec:nmin}\n317\t\n318\tTable~\\ref{tab:nmin} presents the sensitivity of adaptive $b^*$ to the ESS lower bound $n_{\\min}$ (batch size $n = 4$).\n319\t\n320\t\\begin{table}[t]\n321\t\\centering\n322\t\\caption{$n_{\\min}$ sensitivity ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed 42, batch size 4). Only $n_{\\min} = 2$ improves over uniform; $n_{\\min} = 1$ is too aggressive, $n_{\\min} \\geq 3$ collapses to uniform.}\n323\t\\label{tab:nmin}\n324\t\\small\n325\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n326\t\\toprule\n327\t$n_{\\min}$ & Eval PPL & $b^*$ median & Status \\\\\n328\t\\midrule\n329\t1 & 17.710 & 2.60 & Worse than uniform \\\\\n330\t2 & 17.650 & 0.87 & Best \\\\\n331\t3 & 17.690 & $\\sim$0.3 & $\\approx$ Uniform \\\\\n332\t4 & 17.690 & 0.0 & $=$ Uniform \\\\\n333\t5 & 17.690 & 0.0 & $=$ Uniform \\\\\n334\t\\bottomrule\n335\t\\end{tabular}\n336\t\\end{table}\n337\t\n338\tThe sensitivity curve reveals a narrow effective range.\n339\tAt $n_{\\min} = 1$, the ESS constraint is too permissive: $b^*$ reaches median 2.60, contaminated samples receive weight $w \\approx 0.004$, and PPL (17.71) is \\emph{worse} than uniform (17.69) due to over-downweighting.\n340\tAt $n_{\\min} \\geq 3$, the constraint is too tight: $b^*$ is clamped near zero, and the method degrades to uniform training.\n341\tAt $n_{\\min} = 4 = n$ (batch size), $b^* = 0$ identically---the ESS constraint demands all weights be equal, which is only possible when $b = 0$.\n342\t\n343\tOnly $n_{\\min} = 2$ occupies the effective region.\n344\tWhile this single-point sensitivity is a limitation, we note that $n_{\\min}$ selection reduces from a continuous grid search over $b$ (requiring $\\geq 6$ runs) to checking 2--3 integer values, representing a significant practical simplification.\n345\t\n346\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n347\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n348\t\n349\tIn realistic web corpora, synthetic content originates from multiple generator families.\n350\tWe test this by generating contamination with Llama-3.2-1B while training Qwen3-0.6B (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_gen}).\n351\t\n352\t\\begin{table}[t]\n353\t\\centering\n354\t\\caption{Cross-generator evaluation ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed 42). When detector quality degrades on out-of-distribution synthetic text, adaptive reweighting becomes harmful.}\n355\t\\label{tab:cross_gen}\n356\t\\small\n357\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n358\t\\toprule\n359\tSetting & Uniform & Adaptive & $\\Delta$ & Verdict \\\\\n360\t\\midrule\n361\tSelf-gen (Qwen $\\to$ Qwen) & 17.690 & 17.650 & $-$0.040 & Adaptive wins \\\\\n362\tCross-gen (Llama $\\to$ Qwen) & 17.750 & 17.770 & +0.020 & Adaptive \\textbf{loses} \\\\\n363\t\\bottomrule\n364\t\\end{tabular}\n365\t\\end{table}\n366\t\n367\tThe TF-IDF + LR detector, trained only on Qwen3-0.6B-generated text, generalizes poorly to Llama-3.2-1B outputs (AUC drops from 0.939 to 0.891).\n368\tThe $q$-score distribution becomes diffuse (mid-range ratio rises from 0.463 to 0.52), degrading both the $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ estimates and the ranking quality of weights.\n369\tAdaptive $b^*$ (PPL = 17.77) is \\emph{worse} than uniform (17.75): noisy reweighting does more harm than good.\n370\t\n371\tThis exposes a fundamental dependency: adaptive $b^*$ quality is bounded by detector signal-to-noise ratio.\n372\tIn web corpora with diverse synthetic sources, detector AUC may be lower than in controlled settings, making the method less applicable precisely where it would be most needed.\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Injection}\n375\t\\label{sec:domain_skew}\n376\t\n377\tWe test robustness to non-uniform contamination by concentrating 85\\% of synthetic content in the code domain.\n378\t\n379\t\\begin{table}[t]\n380\t\\centering\n381\t\\caption{Domain-skewed contamination ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B). Per-batch adaptation amplifies domain skew rather than correcting it.}\n382\t\\label{tab:domain_skew}\n383\t\\small\n384\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n385\t\\toprule\n386\tMethod & Uniform PPL & Adaptive PPL & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n387\t\\midrule\n388\tUniform injection & 17.690 & 17.650 & $-$0.040 \\\\\n389\tDomain-skewed (code-heavy) & 17.730 & 17.760 & +0.030 \\\\\n390\t\\bottomrule\n391\t\\end{tabular}\n392\t\\end{table}\n393\t\n394\tDomain skew creates a bimodal batch structure: code-heavy batches have uniformly high $q$-scores ($\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ overestimates, $b^*$ too large), while text batches have low $q$-scores ($b^* \\approx 0$).\n395\tThe per-batch mechanism, designed to adapt to local contamination variation, instead amplifies the skew by over-downweighting code batches that may contain useful programming knowledge.\n396\t\n397\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparisons}\n398\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n399\t\n400\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} consolidates all methods under the standard setting ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed 42).\n401\t\n402\t\\begin{table}[t]\n403\t\\centering\n404\t\\caption{Method comparison ($\\rho = 0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed 42). Grid-best $b = 0.2$ dominates; adaptive provides partial improvement; random reweighting confirms detector signal is necessary.}\n405\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n406\t\\small\n407\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n408\t\\toprule\n409\tMethod & PPL & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ Uniform & Signal source \\\\\n410\t\\midrule\n411\tGrid-best ($b = 0.2$) & 17.637 & $-$0.053 & Oracle grid \\\\\n412\tAdaptive $b^*$ & 17.650 & $-$0.040 & Adaptive $q$-based \\\\\n413\tDetect-and-remove ($q > 0.5$) & 17.680 & $-$0.010 & Hard threshold \\\\\n414\tUniform & 17.690 & 0.000 & None \\\\\n415\tRandom reweighting & 17.695 & +0.005 & Random \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tThe ranking is: gentle reweighting ($b = 0.2$) $\\gg$ adaptive $\\gg$ detect-and-remove $>$ uniform $\\approx$ random.\n421\tRandom reweighting (PPL = 17.695 $\\approx$ uniform) confirms that detector-informed signal is load-bearing: arbitrary weight perturbations do not help.\n422\tDetect-and-remove (PPL = 17.68) removes $\\sim$42\\% of data (40\\% true synthetic + 2\\% false positives), losing useful signal that gentle reweighting preserves.\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Downstream Evaluation}\n425\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n426\t\n427\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} reports MMLU and HellaSwag results for Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints.\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[t]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho = 0.4$, seed 42, 200M tokens). No significant differences are observed, consistent with the minimal PPL gap (0.06\\% relative) and severe undertraining (63 tokens/parameter vs.\\ Chinchilla-optimal $\\sim$20$\\times$).}\n432\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n433\t\\small\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\tBenchmark & Uniform & Adaptive & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\tMMLU (5-shot) & 25.8\\% & 25.8\\% & 0.0\\% \\\\\n439\tHellaSwag (10-shot) & 38.2\\% & 38.3\\% & +0.1\\% \\\\\n440\t\\bottomrule\n441\t\\end{tabular}\n442\t\\end{table}\n443\t\n444\tNo significant downstream differences are observed.\n445\tMMLU performance (25.8\\%) is near random-chance level (25\\%), reflecting severe undertraining at 200M tokens for a 3B model (63 tokens/parameter versus $\\sim$20$\\times$ Chinchilla-optimal).\n446\tThe PPL improvement of 0.06\\% relative is far below the resolution of discrete accuracy benchmarks ($\\sim$0.5\\% absolute).\n447\tWe note that PPL is a continuous metric with higher sensitivity than discrete accuracy, and do not claim downstream transfer at current scale.\n448\t\n449\t% ============================================================\n450\t\\section{Discussion}\n451\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n452\t\n453\t\\paragraph{Why adaptive $b^*$ overshoots.}\n454\tThe $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework estimates contamination from $q$-score statistics and seeks the most aggressive downweighting permitted by the ESS constraint.\n455\tThis objective is misaligned with the true optimum in our setting: FineWeb synthetic data generated by Qwen3-0.6B is not pure noise but retains substantial linguistic quality.\n456\tThe optimal strategy ($b = 0.2$, $w = 0.63$) is \\emph{mild correction}, not aggressive removal.\n457\tThe ESS constraint prevents catastrophic over-downweighting (cf.\\ $n_{\\min} = 1$, Table~\\ref{tab:nmin}) but does not guide $b^*$ toward the shallow optimum.\n458\t\n459\t\\paragraph{Diagnostic value of $b^*$ trajectories.}\n460\tDespite numerical inaccuracy, $b^*$ trajectories provide information absent from the Drayson framework.\n461\tThe shift from $b^* = 0.87$ ($\\rho = 0.4$) to $b^* = 1.08$ ($\\rho = 0.6$) correctly indicates increased contamination.\n462\tPer-batch $b^*$ variance reveals contamination heterogeneity: batches with higher contamination receive larger $b^*$, making the framework interpretable for data auditing even when not producing optimal weights.\n463\t\n464\t\\paragraph{When adaptive hurts.}\n465\tWe identify two failure modes: (1) cross-generator contamination, where detector quality degrades (AUC 0.939 $\\to$ 0.891) and noisy $q$-scores lead to harmful reweighting (Section~\\ref{sec:cross_gen}); and (2) domain-skewed contamination, where per-batch adaptation amplifies rather than corrects the skew (Section~\\ref{sec:domain_skew}).\n466\tBoth failure modes stem from violations of implicit assumptions: the detector generalizes well (violated in cross-generator), and contamination is roughly uniform across batches (violated in domain skew).\n467\t\n468\t\\paragraph{The $n_{\\min}$ sensitivity question.}\n469\tA reviewer might argue that replacing grid search over $b$ with selection of $n_{\\min}$ merely substitutes one hyperparameter for another.\n470\tWe partially concede: the method is not hyperparameter-free.\n471\tHowever, $n_{\\min}$ is an integer in $\\{1, \\ldots, n\\}$ (typically 2--3 effective values), requiring far fewer runs to sweep than $b \\in [0.1, 10]$ (our grid uses 9 values).\n472\tThis represents a reduction in tuning cost, not its elimination.\n473\t\n474\t\\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs.\\ SIR.}\n475\tLoss reweighting enables per-batch adaptive $b^*$, which is architecturally incompatible with SIR's per-epoch resampling.\n476\tThe continuous gradient-level control also avoids the discrete variance introduced by stochastic resampling.\n477\tA full gradient variance analysis under both implementations remains future work.\n478\t\n479\t% ============================================================\n480\t\\section{Limitations}\n481\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n482\t\n483\t\\textbf{Scale.}\n484\tAll experiments use 200M tokens, far below production pretraining scale.\n485\tThe 3B model is severely undertrained (63 tokens/parameter).\n486\tEffect sizes at scale may differ qualitatively.\n487\t\n488\t\\textbf{Generator diversity.}\n489\tCross-generator results (Section~\\ref{sec:cross_gen}) demonstrate degradation with a single out-of-distribution generator.\n490\tReal web corpora contain outputs from many generators and model families; robustness under such diversity is untested.\n491\t\n492\t\\textbf{Contamination type.}\n493\tWe study continuation-based synthetic text.\n494\tOther contamination types (paraphrase, translation, summarization, instruction-following outputs) may interact differently with provenance detectors and reweighting schemes.\n495\t\n496\t\\textbf{$n_{\\min}$ sensitivity.}\n497\tThe narrow effective range of $n_{\\min}$ at batch size 4 may not generalize to larger batch sizes, where the integer space grows and the ESS constraint landscape changes.\n498\t\n499\t% ============================================================\n500\t\\section{Conclusion}\n501\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n502\t\n503\tWe presented adaptive $b^*$, an online method for deriving the weighting exponent in provenance-weighted pretraining from per-batch contamination statistics.\n504\tOur investigation yields three actionable findings: (1) lightweight detectors (TF-IDF + LR) suffice for provenance weighting, with 3000$\\times$ fewer parameters and negligible PPL difference compared to neural alternatives; (2) the $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework provides batch-level contamination diagnostics, though its derived $b^*$ systematically overshoots the grid optimum by $\\sim$4$\\times$; and (3) adaptive $b^*$ serves as a zero-cost reasonable default recovering $\\sim$70\\% of grid-best improvement, while failing under cross-generator and domain-skewed contamination.\n505\tThese results suggest that future work should focus on closing the overshoot gap---potentially through model-aware corrections to $b^*$---and on improving detector robustness to diverse synthetic sources at scale.\n506\t\n507\t% ============================================================\n508\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n509\t\n510\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n511\t\n512\t\\bibitem[Drayson et~al.(2025)]{drayson2025contamination}\n513\tDrayson, O., et~al.\n514\t\\newblock Detecting and mitigating model-generated text in internet corpora.\n515\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2025.\n516\t\n517\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024strong}\n518\tShumailov, I., et~al.\n519\t\\newblock AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n520\t\\newblock In \\emph{Nature}, 2024.\n521\t\n522\t\\bibitem[Dohmatob et~al.(2025)]{dohmatob2025strong}\n523\tDohmatob, E., et~al.\n524\t\\newblock Strong model collapse.\n525\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n526\t\n527\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023)]{xie2023dsir}\n528\tXie, S.~M., et~al.\n529\t\\newblock Data selection for language models via importance resampling.\n530\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n531\t\n532\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023b)]{xie2023doremi}\n533\tXie, S.~M., et~al.\n534\t\\newblock DoReMi: Optimizing data mixtures by domain reweighting.\n535\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n536\t\n537\t\\bibitem[Abbas et~al.(2024)]{abbas2024softdedup}\n538\tAbbas, A., et~al.\n539\t\\newblock SoftDedup: Soft deduplication for training data.\n540\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n541\t\n542\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2025)]{li2025goldenratio}\n543\tLi, Y., et~al.\n544\t\\newblock Golden ratio weighting for synthetic and human data.\n545\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.18049}, 2025.\n546\t\n547\t\\bibitem[Anonymous(2025)]{convergence2025}\n548\tAnonymous.\n549\t\\newblock Convergence bounds for contaminated distributions."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"862dd338-7e6c-44c2-9c06-6935bbbbfbd7","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:22:05.846Z"}
