{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Akh2CDcXVhnfXAUq7Yj5ZR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D9fEQavoMRc7EEuEx9PJaV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/best/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3450,"cache_read_input_tokens":55294,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3450,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTFXWJ17ucgV9rhcawcSwM","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"623aaf55-0c35-40cc-ac23-f4e1ea7fce70"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D9fEQavoMRc7EEuEx9PJaV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\tWeb corpora used for language model pretraining increasingly contain synthetic text of unknown provenance. Detect-and-reweight methods assign sample weights $w_i=(1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search on a clean validation set. We propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: a per-batch estimator that derives the optimal exponent from an effective tail-index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of the within-batch score distribution, subject to an effective-sample-size (ESS) constraint. The method requires no validation set, no grid search, and no knowledge of the contamination proportion. Across two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Llama-3.2-3B), two contamination rates (40\\%, 60\\%), cross-generator injection, domain-skewed contamination, and two detector qualities, adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds the grid-search optimum (aggregate ratio $\\geq$88.6\\% at $\\rho$=0.4; surpasses grid-best at $\\rho$=0.6 and on 3B). The $b^*$ trajectory shifts automatically with contamination rate and model scale, providing direct evidence that the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework captures the correct data-driven statistics. Effect amplification from 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement (0.6B, $\\rho$=0.4) to 1.24\\% (3B, $\\rho$=0.4) addresses the small-effect-size concern. Downstream gains on MMLU (+0.7\\%) and HellaSwag (+1.1\\%) confirm transfer beyond perplexity.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t%==============================================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\n35\tLarge language models are pretrained on web-scraped corpora that increasingly contain machine-generated text. Estimates suggest 20--60\\% of web text may be synthetically generated, and the Strong Model Collapse result~\\citep{shumailov2024strong} demonstrates that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws. This motivates principled methods for contamination-robust pretraining.\n36\t\n37\t\\citet{drayson2025contamination} introduced the detect-and-reweight paradigm: given per-sample contamination scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$ from a binary detector, assign weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ and resample the training data via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR). Their approach is effective but requires the exponent $b$ to be selected empirically per model (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2 $\\leq$360M, $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B) using a clean validation set---a resource that is unavailable in large-scale web pretraining.\n38\t\n39\tWe make two observations. First, the convergence theory of~\\citet{convergence2025} provides a bound $d(\\hat{\\mathbb{P}}_t, \\mathbb{P}_0) \\lesssim t^{-\\min\\{p,\\alpha\\}}$ that connects the tail index $\\alpha$ of the data distribution to the rate at which reweighted training converges to the clean distribution. This motivates defining an \\emph{effective tail index} $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ as a function of the weight exponent. Second, the effective sample size (ESS) of a weighted batch provides a natural constraint against over-aggressive downweighting.\n40\t\n41\tWe propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: for each training batch, we estimate $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ from the empirical distribution of $q$-scores, then select $b^*$ to maximize $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ subject to ESS $\\geq n_{\\min}$. This replaces grid search with a closed-form per-batch computation, eliminates the need for a validation set, and adapts automatically to varying contamination levels within and across datasets.\n42\t\n43\tOur contributions are:\n44\t\\begin{enumerate}\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical framework.} We introduce $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ estimation with ESS constraints as a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent, replacing per-model empirical tuning.\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical result.} Adaptive $b^*$ matches or exceeds grid-search best across model scales, contamination rates, generators, and detector qualities, at zero tuning cost.\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Problem setting.} Unlike Golden Ratio Weighting (which requires known contamination proportion $k$) and unlike validation-based $b$ selection, our method operates with unknown contamination proportion using only detector scores.\n48\t\\end{enumerate}\n49\t\n50\t%=============================================================================="}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTFXWJ17ucgV9rhcawcSwM","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"1bb3283d-8ad2-45a9-bacf-28801b25ddd9","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:22:05.975Z"}
