{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N717tbwdPwznU9ZdsPCa4e","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of both the \"best\" and \"worst\" framings of the paper, the code, the internal decision history, and the experimental data. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\n**Reviewer R1 — Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**The alpha_eff(b) framework is under-specified as a \"theoretical\" contribution.** The paper claims to derive b* from an effective tail index motivated by the convergence bound d(P_hat_t, P_0) <= t^{-min{p,alpha}}. However, the actual connection between the cited convergence theory and the alpha_eff(b) definition is hand-waved. The alpha_eff(b) as defined in the code (line 29 of `alpha_eff.py`) is simply a weighted mean of (1-q): alpha_eff(b) = sum((1-q)^{b+1}) / sum((1-q)^b). This is not a tail index in any standard statistical sense (Hill estimator, Pickands, etc.). It is a reweighted average authenticity score. Calling it a \"tail index\" or claiming it is motivated by convergence theory when no formal derivation connects the two is misleading.\n\n**The optimization objective is misaligned with the goal.** The paper defines b* = argmax_b alpha_eff(b) s.t. ESS >= n_min. Since alpha_eff(b) is monotonically non-decreasing in b (as the code verifies), the \"optimal\" b* is always the largest b permitted by the ESS constraint. This means the ESS constraint is the *sole* operative mechanism---the alpha_eff framework contributes nothing to b* selection beyond providing a monotone function to maximize. The b* derivation could equivalently be stated as \"pick the largest b such that ESS(b) >= n_min,\" rendering the theoretical apparatus unnecessary.\n\n**The ESS constraint is principled but the choice of n_min is fragile.** ESS as a regularizer against over-downweighting is a reasonable idea. However, with batch_size=4 and n_min=2, the ESS dynamic range is [2, 4]---a 2x range. The method operates at the boundary of its constraint for ~75% of batches (ESS ≈ 2.0, per project memory). This is not \"adaptive per-batch b*\" in any meaningful sense; it is essentially picking the maximum b before ESS degenerates, which happens to be roughly the same value for most batches.\n\n**Loss reweighting vs. SIR comparison is claimed but not delivered.** The intro lists this as a contribution, but no experiment compares the two implementations. The paper discusses differences at a conceptual level only.\n\n### 2. Experimental Design\n\n**Grid search was initially incomplete and retroactively extended.** The internal decision log (D019, ROADMAP.md) reveals that the project initially used b in {0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0} with grid-best b=0.5. When this yielded an aggregate ratio of 88.6%, the grid was extended to b in {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, ...} and the grid-best shifted to b=0.2, dropping the recovery ratio to 69.8% (worst-case framing) or the lower b values were retroactively inserted (best-case framing adds b=0.1/0.2/0.3 to the same table). This retroactive grid expansion is methodologically appropriate but the paper does not discuss how the grid was chosen, leaving the reader unaware that the initial grid missed the optimum.\n\n**The \"best\" and \"worst\" paper framings present contradictory numbers.** The best-case paper claims grid-best b=0.5 (Table 1 stops at b=0.5), 88.6% recovery, rho=0.6 amplification to 1.06%, and 3B improvement of 1.24%. The worst-case paper adds b=0.1/0.2/0.3 rows, reveals grid-best b=0.2, shows 69.8% recovery, rho=0.6 effect *not* amplifying (Delta drops from 0.04 to 0.03), and 3B showing only 0.06% improvement. The discrepancies between these framings are substantial and suggest the \"best-case\" paper selectively truncates the grid to inflate the recovery ratio.\n\n**Cross-generator and domain-skewed results differ wildly between framings.** The best-case paper claims adaptive *improves* by 0.05 PPL in both cross-gen and domain-skewed settings. The worst-case paper shows adaptive *loses* to uniform in both (+0.02 and +0.03 respectively). These cannot both be correct. This raises serious concerns about data fabrication in one of the two framings. Given that the internal project notes never mention cross-generator or domain-skewed experiments being completed (they appear only in the dream report), the most likely explanation is that these results are simulated/projected rather than empirically measured.\n\n**Single-seed results dominate.** The rho=0.6 comparison, cross-model scaling, cross-generator, domain-skew, and downstream evaluations all report single-seed (seed=42) results. For effect sizes this small, single-seed results are scientifically meaningless.\n\n**Downstream evaluation is uninformative.** MMLU at 25.8% is random-chance level (25% for 4-choice), confirming the model is catastrophically undertrained at 200M tokens for 3B parameters (63 tokens/parameter vs. ~1200 Chinchilla-optimal). The best-case paper's claimed +0.7% MMLU and +1.1% HellaSwag improvements are flatly contradicted by the worst-case paper's 0.0% and +0.1% respectively. At least one set of numbers is fabricated.\n\n### 3. Statistical Rigor\n\n**Effect sizes are below meaningful thresholds throughout.** The primary result is a PPL difference of 0.040 on a base of ~17.69 (0.23% relative). For context:\n- Cross-seed variance in uniform baselines spans 0.010 PPL (17.690/17.690/17.700)\n- The adaptive improvement (0.040) is only 4x the seed variance\n- After grid extension to b=0.2, the adaptive-vs-grid gap (0.013) is only ~1.5x seed variance\n\n**No confidence intervals or significance tests on the primary claim.** The paper reports 3-seed results but provides no confidence intervals, no paired tests between adaptive and uniform, and no bootstrap intervals. A paired t-test on 3 data points has very low power. The only significance test reported (b=0.3 vs b=0.5, p=0.42) actually demonstrates that the grid landscape is flat within noise.\n\n**The \"effect amplification\" claim is questionable.** The best-case paper claims 4.75x amplification at rho=0.6 and 5.39x at 3B. The worst-case paper shows the opposite: rho=0.6 improvement *decreases* from 0.04 to 0.03, and 3B improvement is only 0.01 PPL. These contradictions must be resolved. If the worst-case numbers are more reliable (consistent with the project's actual experimental logs showing 3B improvement of 0.01 PPL in the ROADMAP), the amplification claim is false.\n\n**Recovery ratio arithmetic is fragile.** At seed456, the adaptive PPL is 17.660 vs. grid-best 17.650 vs. uniform 17.700. The \"80% recovery\" ratio is (17.700-17.660)/(17.700-17.650) = 0.040/0.050 = 80%. But a 0.010 PPL difference determines whether recovery is 80% or 100%. This is within plausible noise.\n\n### 4. Reproducibility\n\n**Code is provided and well-structured.** The `alpha_eff.py` and `pretrain_weighted.py` files are clean, commented, and implement the claimed algorithm correctly. The `find_optimal_b` function uses a 1000-point grid + bisection refinement, which is reproducible.\n\n**Key details are specified**: batch size 4, gradient accumulation 4, learning rate 5e-5, warmup 0.05, weight decay 0.01, max_length 2048, 6103 training steps, seed values.\n\n**Missing details**: The paper does not specify the specific FineWeb subset used (which date range, filtering criteria). The synthetic data generation process (continuation-based, but prompt format, temperature, top-p/k settings) is underspecified. The TF-IDF detector's feature configuration (ngram range, max features, vocabulary size) is not provided.\n\n**tau computation is undisclosed in the paper.** The code reveals tau = 1.0 - contamination_ratio * 0.5 = 0.8 (for rho=0.4). This threshold directly affects b* computation through the find_optimal_b function. The paper describes b* as maximizing alpha_eff subject to ESS, but the code actually finds the *smallest* b where alpha_eff >= tau OR the largest feasible b if tau is unreachable. This discrepancy between Algorithm 1 in the paper and the actual implementation is significant.\n\n### 5. Top 5 Weaknesses\n\n**W1: The alpha_eff \"theoretical framework\" adds no value to b* selection.** Since alpha_eff is monotonically non-decreasing, maximizing alpha_eff s.t. ESS >= n_min always selects the largest feasible b. The entire theoretical apparatus is a complex way to say \"maximize b subject to ESS >= n_min.\" The tau threshold in the code adds a secondary criterion not described in the main paper's Algorithm 1.\n\n**W2: Contradictory results between paper framings.** The \"best\" and \"worst\" dream reports present materially different experimental numbers for the same experiments. Cross-gen results flip from +0.05 improvement to -0.02 regression. Downstream results flip from +0.7% MMLU to +0.0%. At least one set is fabricated or simulated. This undermines trust in all reported numbers.\n\n**W3: Effect sizes are at or below noise floor.** The core improvement (0.040 PPL, 0.23% relative) is marginally meaningful. After extending the grid to include b=0.2, the advantage of adaptive over grid-best shrinks to 0.013 PPL (0.07% relative), which is within seed variance (std ~0.007). The claim that adaptive \"matches\" grid-best is generous; more accurately, both adaptive and grid-best produce improvements indistinguishable from noise over uniform.\n\n**W4: n_min is a fragile hyperparameter with an extremely narrow sweet spot.** With batch_size=4, only n_min=2 works. n_min=1 is over-aggressive (PPL worse than uniform in the worst-case framing), n_min>=3 degenerates to uniform. This \"hyperparameter-free\" method has exchanged one difficult hyperparameter (b) for another (n_min) with a narrower effective range, and additionally depends on batch_size (since n_min must be < batch_size for any effect). The paper's argument that \"n_min reduces from continuous to integer\" is undercut by the fact that only a single integer value works.\n\n**W5: Evaluation scale is insufficient for the claims.** 200M tokens / 0.6B parameters is a toy setting for pretraining research. The 3B experiments at 200M tokens (63 tokens/parameter) are so severely undertrained that downstream metrics are at chance level. The paper's claims about \"web-scale pretraining\" applicability are unsupported by experiments at this scale.\n\n### 6. Strengths\n\n**S1: Problem identification is well-motivated.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model validation-based tuning, and that this is impractical for web-scale pretraining, is a real problem worth solving.\n\n**S2: Honest self-assessment (in the worst-case framing).** The worst-case paper's characterization of adaptive b* as a \"contamination thermometer, not an optimizer\" that \"overshoots by ~4x\" and serves as a \"reasonable default recovering ~70%\" is refreshingly honest. The identification of failure modes (cross-gen, domain-skew) strengthens the contribution.\n\n**S3: Lightweight detector finding is practically valuable.** The demonstration that TF-IDF+LR achieves near-equivalent training outcomes to ModernBERT-150M, if confirmed with multiple seeds, would be a genuinely useful finding for practitioners.\n\n**S4: Code quality and experimental logging.** The codebase is clean, the decision log is thorough, and the experimental methodology (3 seeds, held-out eval, controlled injection) follows good practices within the scale constraints.\n\n**S5: Ablation coverage is thorough.** The n_min sweep, detector quality comparison, contamination rate variation, cross-generator test, and domain-skew test represent a comprehensive ablation suite for a method paper.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The code's `find_optimal_b` uses a tau threshold (alpha_eff >= tau) that is not described in Algorithm 1 of the paper. Algorithm 1 says \"argmax alpha_eff s.t. ESS >= n_min,\" while the code finds inf{b: alpha_eff(b) >= tau} s.t. ESS >= n_min, falling back to the ESS-constrained maximum if tau is unreachable. Which is the actual method? How sensitive is the method to tau?\n\n**Q2.** Given that alpha_eff(b) is monotonically non-decreasing in b, why frame b* selection as \"maximizing alpha_eff\"? The ESS constraint alone determines b*. What does the alpha_eff framework contribute beyond the ESS constraint?\n\n**Q3.** The cross-generator and domain-skewed results differ dramatically between the two paper framings (+0.05 improvement vs. -0.02 regression; +0.05 vs +0.03 harm). Which numbers are from actual experiments? Were these experiments run with 3 seeds?\n\n**Q4.** With batch_size=4 and n_min=2, ESS touches 2.0 for ~75% of batches. Have you run experiments with larger batch sizes (e.g., 16, 64) where the ESS dynamic range would be wider? The current setting is at the minimum resolution for the method to operate.\n\n**Q5.** The paper does not compare loss reweighting vs. SIR despite listing this as a contribution. Can you provide an empirical comparison, or should this claim be removed?\n\n### 8. Score\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a real problem (automated b selection for contamination reweighting) but the proposed solution has fundamental issues. The \"theoretical framework\" (alpha_eff) is functionally vacuous---b* is entirely determined by the ESS constraint, not by alpha_eff. Effect sizes are at the noise floor throughout. The method introduces a new hyperparameter (n_min) with a narrower effective range than the one it replaces. Two of the three claimed contributions are either unsubstantiated (loss reweighting vs. SIR comparison) or misleading (the alpha_eff \"theoretical framework\"). The contradictions between the two paper framings raise concerns about which experimental results are real vs. projected.\n\nFor a top ML venue at ~25% acceptance, the contribution needs to be either theoretically novel (the alpha_eff framework is not, once one recognizes it reduces to an ESS threshold) or empirically compelling (0.04 PPL at 200M token scale is not). The honest, worst-case framing that positions this as a \"diagnostic tool and reasonable default\" is more defensible than the best-case framing, but even that reduced claim struggles to clear the bar given the n_min fragility and failure under cross-generator / domain-skewed conditions.\n\n### 9. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nI have read the paper in full (both framings), examined the code implementation of alpha_eff.py and pretrain_weighted.py, reviewed the internal decision logs (D001-D019), ROADMAP, memory.md, and paper_framing.md. The critical finding that alpha_eff maximization reduces to ESS thresholding is based on direct code analysis and the mathematical monotonicity property noted in the code itself. The contradiction between paper framings is clear from textual comparison. I am confident in the technical assessment, though I note uncertainty about which experimental numbers are from completed runs vs. simulated projections."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6232868e7dde7c22 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6232868e7dde7c22' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 75108\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 175848</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"5d0253f3-9909-4ce8-9fe7-e7a4354a3f5c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:23:54.141Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a blind peer review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Adaptive Provenance Weighting for Contaminated Pretraining Corpora\" proposes adaptive b*, an online method that derives the weighting exponent b in provenance-weighted pretraining (w_i = (1-q_i)^b) from per-batch contamination statistics α̂_eff(b) subject to an ESS constraint, requiring no validation set. Three claimed contributions:\n1. Lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) achieves within 0.003 PPL of ModernBERT 150M (AUC=0.986)\n2. α̂_eff(b) framework as diagnostic tool — b* systematically overshoots grid-optimal by ~4× (median b*≈0.87 vs grid-best b=0.2), but b* trajectory reveals batch-level contamination structure\n3. Adaptive b* as zero-cost reasonable default recovering ~70% of grid-best improvement, degrading under cross-generator and domain-skewed scenarios\n\nAdditionally: loss reweighting vs SIR comparison (claimed contribution #3 in intro).\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n- Data: FineWeb 200M tokens, ρ∈{0.4, 0.6}, synthetic via Qwen3-0.6B (self-gen) or Llama-3.2-1B (cross-gen)\n- Models: Qwen3-0.6B (primary), Llama-3.2-3B (scale test)\n- Eval: PPL on ~4M token holdout; MMLU/HellaSwag for 3B\n- Grid: b∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5,1.0,1.5,2.0,3.0,5.0}, 3 seeds (42,123,456)\n- Baselines: uniform, detect-and-remove (q>0.5), random reweighting\n- n_min sensitivity: {1,2,3,4,5} with batch_size=4\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: Full grid PPL curve. Grid-best b=0.2 across all seeds. PPL differences are tiny (best 17.625, worst uniform 17.700, range 0.075)\n- Table 2: Aggregate recovery ratio 69.8%\n- Table 3: TF-IDF+LR vs ModernBERT: PPL diff only 0.003\n- Table 4: ρ=0.4 vs 0.6: effect does NOT amplify (Δ drops from 0.04 to 0.03)\n- Table 5: 0.6B vs 3B: b* nearly identical (KS p=0.87), 3B improvement only 0.01 PPL\n- Table 6: n_min sensitivity — only n_min=2 works, narrow sweet spot\n- Table 7: Cross-gen: adaptive LOSES (+0.02 vs uniform)\n- Table 8: Domain-skewed: adaptive LOSES (+0.03 vs uniform)\n- Table 9: Method ranking: grid-best >> adaptive >> detect-remove > uniform ≈ random\n- Table 10: Downstream (MMLU/HellaSwag): no significant differences\n\n## Experiment Coverage Check\nThe review context lists these planned experiments. Check which appear in the paper:\n- Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3) for 3 seeds ✓ (Table 1)\n- Post-hoc eval for 9 low-b checkpoints ✓ (Table 1)\n- ρ=0.6 data generation ✓ (implicit)\n- ρ=0.6 uniform baseline ✓ (Table 4)\n- ρ=0.6 adaptive ✓ (Table 4)\n- ρ=0.6 grid b=0.5 ✓ (mentioned in Section 5.3)\n- Llama-3.2-3B setup ✓ (implicit)\n- Llama-3.2-3B uniform ✓ (Table 5, Table 10)\n- Llama-3.2-3B adaptive ✓ (Table 5, Table 10)\n- n_min=1 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- n_min=4 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- n_min=5 ablation ✓ (Table 6)\n- ModernBERT detector ✓ (Table 3)\n- ModernBERT training ablation ✓ (Table 3)\n- Cross-generator datagen ✓ (Section 5.6)\n- Cross-generator training ✓ (Table 7)\n- Detect-and-remove baseline ✓ (Table 9)\n- Random reweighting baseline ✓ (Table 9)\n- Domain-skewed injection ✓ (Table 8)\n- Downstream eval ✓ (Table 10)\n\nAll 20 planned experiments appear in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** — Is the adaptive b* derivation well-motivated? Are the mathematical formulations correct? Is the ESS constraint principled?\n\n2. **Experimental Design** — Are experiments well-controlled? Are comparisons fair? Are there missing baselines or ablations?\n\n3. **Statistical Rigor** — Are effect sizes meaningful? Is seed variance properly accounted for? Are claims supported by the data?\n\n4. **Reproducibility** — Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — Be specific. Identify the top 3-5 weaknesses with concrete evidence from the paper.\n\n6. **Strengths** — What does the paper do well?\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** — 3-5 specific questions\n\n8. **Score** — 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n9. **Confidence** — 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. The PPL differences throughout this paper are extremely small (often <0.05 absolute on a base of ~17.7). Consider whether these are meaningful.\n","agentId":"a6232868e7dde7c22","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of both the \"best\" and \"worst\" framings of the paper, the code, the internal decision history, and the experimental data. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Adaptive Provenance Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\n**Reviewer R1 — Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**The alpha_eff(b) framework is under-specified as a \"theoretical\" contribution.** The paper claims to derive b* from an effective tail index motivated by the convergence bound d(P_hat_t, P_0) <= t^{-min{p,alpha}}. However, the actual connection between the cited convergence theory and the alpha_eff(b) definition is hand-waved. The alpha_eff(b) as defined in the code (line 29 of `alpha_eff.py`) is simply a weighted mean of (1-q): alpha_eff(b) = sum((1-q)^{b+1}) / sum((1-q)^b). This is not a tail index in any standard statistical sense (Hill estimator, Pickands, etc.). It is a reweighted average authenticity score. Calling it a \"tail index\" or claiming it is motivated by convergence theory when no formal derivation connects the two is misleading.\n\n**The optimization objective is misaligned with the goal.** The paper defines b* = argmax_b alpha_eff(b) s.t. ESS >= n_min. Since alpha_eff(b) is monotonically non-decreasing in b (as the code verifies), the \"optimal\" b* is always the largest b permitted by the ESS constraint. This means the ESS constraint is the *sole* operative mechanism---the alpha_eff framework contributes nothing to b* selection beyond providing a monotone function to maximize. The b* derivation could equivalently be stated as \"pick the largest b such that ESS(b) >= n_min,\" rendering the theoretical apparatus unnecessary.\n\n**The ESS constraint is principled but the choice of n_min is fragile.** ESS as a regularizer against over-downweighting is a reasonable idea. However, with batch_size=4 and n_min=2, the ESS dynamic range is [2, 4]---a 2x range. The method operates at the boundary of its constraint for ~75% of batches (ESS ≈ 2.0, per project memory). This is not \"adaptive per-batch b*\" in any meaningful sense; it is essentially picking the maximum b before ESS degenerates, which happens to be roughly the same value for most batches.\n\n**Loss reweighting vs. SIR comparison is claimed but not delivered.** The intro lists this as a contribution, but no experiment compares the two implementations. The paper discusses differences at a conceptual level only.\n\n### 2. Experimental Design\n\n**Grid search was initially incomplete and retroactively extended.** The internal decision log (D019, ROADMAP.md) reveals that the project initially used b in {0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0} with grid-best b=0.5. When this yielded an aggregate ratio of 88.6%, the grid was extended to b in {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, ...} and the grid-best shifted to b=0.2, dropping the recovery ratio to 69.8% (worst-case framing) or the lower b values were retroactively inserted (best-case framing adds b=0.1/0.2/0.3 to the same table). This retroactive grid expansion is methodologically appropriate but the paper does not discuss how the grid was chosen, leaving the reader unaware that the initial grid missed the optimum.\n\n**The \"best\" and \"worst\" paper framings present contradictory numbers.** The best-case paper claims grid-best b=0.5 (Table 1 stops at b=0.5), 88.6% recovery, rho=0.6 amplification to 1.06%, and 3B improvement of 1.24%. The worst-case paper adds b=0.1/0.2/0.3 rows, reveals grid-best b=0.2, shows 69.8% recovery, rho=0.6 effect *not* amplifying (Delta drops from 0.04 to 0.03), and 3B showing only 0.06% improvement. The discrepancies between these framings are substantial and suggest the \"best-case\" paper selectively truncates the grid to inflate the recovery ratio.\n\n**Cross-generator and domain-skewed results differ wildly between framings.** The best-case paper claims adaptive *improves* by 0.05 PPL in both cross-gen and domain-skewed settings. The worst-case paper shows adaptive *loses* to uniform in both (+0.02 and +0.03 respectively). These cannot both be correct. This raises serious concerns about data fabrication in one of the two framings. Given that the internal project notes never mention cross-generator or domain-skewed experiments being completed (they appear only in the dream report), the most likely explanation is that these results are simulated/projected rather than empirically measured.\n\n**Single-seed results dominate.** The rho=0.6 comparison, cross-model scaling, cross-generator, domain-skew, and downstream evaluations all report single-seed (seed=42) results. For effect sizes this small, single-seed results are scientifically meaningless.\n\n**Downstream evaluation is uninformative.** MMLU at 25.8% is random-chance level (25% for 4-choice), confirming the model is catastrophically undertrained at 200M tokens for 3B parameters (63 tokens/parameter vs. ~1200 Chinchilla-optimal). The best-case paper's claimed +0.7% MMLU and +1.1% HellaSwag improvements are flatly contradicted by the worst-case paper's 0.0% and +0.1% respectively. At least one set of numbers is fabricated.\n\n### 3. Statistical Rigor\n\n**Effect sizes are below meaningful thresholds throughout.** The primary result is a PPL difference of 0.040 on a base of ~17.69 (0.23% relative). For context:\n- Cross-seed variance in uniform baselines spans 0.010 PPL (17.690/17.690/17.700)\n- The adaptive improvement (0.040) is only 4x the seed variance\n- After grid extension to b=0.2, the adaptive-vs-grid gap (0.013) is only ~1.5x seed variance\n\n**No confidence intervals or significance tests on the primary claim.** The paper reports 3-seed results but provides no confidence intervals, no paired tests between adaptive and uniform, and no bootstrap intervals. A paired t-test on 3 data points has very low power. The only significance test reported (b=0.3 vs b=0.5, p=0.42) actually demonstrates that the grid landscape is flat within noise.\n\n**The \"effect amplification\" claim is questionable.** The best-case paper claims 4.75x amplification at rho=0.6 and 5.39x at 3B. The worst-case paper shows the opposite: rho=0.6 improvement *decreases* from 0.04 to 0.03, and 3B improvement is only 0.01 PPL. These contradictions must be resolved. If the worst-case numbers are more reliable (consistent with the project's actual experimental logs showing 3B improvement of 0.01 PPL in the ROADMAP), the amplification claim is false.\n\n**Recovery ratio arithmetic is fragile.** At seed456, the adaptive PPL is 17.660 vs. grid-best 17.650 vs. uniform 17.700. The \"80% recovery\" ratio is (17.700-17.660)/(17.700-17.650) = 0.040/0.050 = 80%. But a 0.010 PPL difference determines whether recovery is 80% or 100%. This is within plausible noise.\n\n### 4. Reproducibility\n\n**Code is provided and well-structured.** The `alpha_eff.py` and `pretrain_weighted.py` files are clean, commented, and implement the claimed algorithm correctly. The `find_optimal_b` function uses a 1000-point grid + bisection refinement, which is reproducible.\n\n**Key details are specified**: batch size 4, gradient accumulation 4, learning rate 5e-5, warmup 0.05, weight decay 0.01, max_length 2048, 6103 training steps, seed values.\n\n**Missing details**: The paper does not specify the specific FineWeb subset used (which date range, filtering criteria). The synthetic data generation process (continuation-based, but prompt format, temperature, top-p/k settings) is underspecified. The TF-IDF detector's feature configuration (ngram range, max features, vocabulary size) is not provided.\n\n**tau computation is undisclosed in the paper.** The code reveals tau = 1.0 - contamination_ratio * 0.5 = 0.8 (for rho=0.4). This threshold directly affects b* computation through the find_optimal_b function. The paper describes b* as maximizing alpha_eff subject to ESS, but the code actually finds the *smallest* b where alpha_eff >= tau OR the largest feasible b if tau is unreachable. This discrepancy between Algorithm 1 in the paper and the actual implementation is significant.\n\n### 5. Top 5 Weaknesses\n\n**W1: The alpha_eff \"theoretical framework\" adds no value to b* selection.** Since alpha_eff is monotonically non-decreasing, maximizing alpha_eff s.t. ESS >= n_min always selects the largest feasible b. The entire theoretical apparatus is a complex way to say \"maximize b subject to ESS >= n_min.\" The tau threshold in the code adds a secondary criterion not described in the main paper's Algorithm 1.\n\n**W2: Contradictory results between paper framings.** The \"best\" and \"worst\" dream reports present materially different experimental numbers for the same experiments. Cross-gen results flip from +0.05 improvement to -0.02 regression. Downstream results flip from +0.7% MMLU to +0.0%. At least one set is fabricated or simulated. This undermines trust in all reported numbers.\n\n**W3: Effect sizes are at or below noise floor.** The core improvement (0.040 PPL, 0.23% relative) is marginally meaningful. After extending the grid to include b=0.2, the advantage of adaptive over grid-best shrinks to 0.013 PPL (0.07% relative), which is within seed variance (std ~0.007). The claim that adaptive \"matches\" grid-best is generous; more accurately, both adaptive and grid-best produce improvements indistinguishable from noise over uniform.\n\n**W4: n_min is a fragile hyperparameter with an extremely narrow sweet spot.** With batch_size=4, only n_min=2 works. n_min=1 is over-aggressive (PPL worse than uniform in the worst-case framing), n_min>=3 degenerates to uniform. This \"hyperparameter-free\" method has exchanged one difficult hyperparameter (b) for another (n_min) with a narrower effective range, and additionally depends on batch_size (since n_min must be < batch_size for any effect). The paper's argument that \"n_min reduces from continuous to integer\" is undercut by the fact that only a single integer value works.\n\n**W5: Evaluation scale is insufficient for the claims.** 200M tokens / 0.6B parameters is a toy setting for pretraining research. The 3B experiments at 200M tokens (63 tokens/parameter) are so severely undertrained that downstream metrics are at chance level. The paper's claims about \"web-scale pretraining\" applicability are unsupported by experiments at this scale.\n\n### 6. Strengths\n\n**S1: Problem identification is well-motivated.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model validation-based tuning, and that this is impractical for web-scale pretraining, is a real problem worth solving.\n\n**S2: Honest self-assessment (in the worst-case framing).** The worst-case paper's characterization of adaptive b* as a \"contamination thermometer, not an optimizer\" that \"overshoots by ~4x\" and serves as a \"reasonable default recovering ~70%\" is refreshingly honest. The identification of failure modes (cross-gen, domain-skew) strengthens the contribution.\n\n**S3: Lightweight detector finding is practically valuable.** The demonstration that TF-IDF+LR achieves near-equivalent training outcomes to ModernBERT-150M, if confirmed with multiple seeds, would be a genuinely useful finding for practitioners.\n\n**S4: Code quality and experimental logging.** The codebase is clean, the decision log is thorough, and the experimental methodology (3 seeds, held-out eval, controlled injection) follows good practices within the scale constraints.\n\n**S5: Ablation coverage is thorough.** The n_min sweep, detector quality comparison, contamination rate variation, cross-generator test, and domain-skew test represent a comprehensive ablation suite for a method paper.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The code's `find_optimal_b` uses a tau threshold (alpha_eff >= tau) that is not described in Algorithm 1 of the paper. Algorithm 1 says \"argmax alpha_eff s.t. ESS >= n_min,\" while the code finds inf{b: alpha_eff(b) >= tau} s.t. ESS >= n_min, falling back to the ESS-constrained maximum if tau is unreachable. Which is the actual method? How sensitive is the method to tau?\n\n**Q2.** Given that alpha_eff(b) is monotonically non-decreasing in b, why frame b* selection as \"maximizing alpha_eff\"? The ESS constraint alone determines b*. What does the alpha_eff framework contribute beyond the ESS constraint?\n\n**Q3.** The cross-generator and domain-skewed results differ dramatically between the two paper framings (+0.05 improvement vs. -0.02 regression; +0.05 vs +0.03 harm). Which numbers are from actual experiments? Were these experiments run with 3 seeds?\n\n**Q4.** With batch_size=4 and n_min=2, ESS touches 2.0 for ~75% of batches. Have you run experiments with larger batch sizes (e.g., 16, 64) where the ESS dynamic range would be wider? The current setting is at the minimum resolution for the method to operate.\n\n**Q5.** The paper does not compare loss reweighting vs. SIR despite listing this as a contribution. Can you provide an empirical comparison, or should this claim be removed?\n\n### 8. Score\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a real problem (automated b selection for contamination reweighting) but the proposed solution has fundamental issues. The \"theoretical framework\" (alpha_eff) is functionally vacuous---b* is entirely determined by the ESS constraint, not by alpha_eff. Effect sizes are at the noise floor throughout. The method introduces a new hyperparameter (n_min) with a narrower effective range than the one it replaces. Two of the three claimed contributions are either unsubstantiated (loss reweighting vs. SIR comparison) or misleading (the alpha_eff \"theoretical framework\"). The contradictions between the two paper framings raise concerns about which experimental results are real vs. projected.\n\nFor a top ML venue at ~25% acceptance, the contribution needs to be either theoretically novel (the alpha_eff framework is not, once one recognizes it reduces to an ESS threshold) or empirically compelling (0.04 PPL at 200M token scale is not). The honest, worst-case framing that positions this as a \"diagnostic tool and reasonable default\" is more defensible than the best-case framing, but even that reduced claim struggles to clear the bar given the n_min fragility and failure under cross-generator / domain-skewed conditions.\n\n### 9. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nI have read the paper in full (both framings), examined the code implementation of alpha_eff.py and pretrain_weighted.py, reviewed the internal decision logs (D001-D019), ROADMAP, memory.md, and paper_framing.md. The critical finding that alpha_eff maximization reduces to ESS thresholding is based on direct code analysis and the mathematical monotonicity property noted in the code itself. The contradiction between paper framings is clear from textual comparison. I am confident in the technical assessment, though I note uncertainty about which experimental numbers are from completed runs vs. simulated projections."}],"totalDurationMs":175848,"totalTokens":75108,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6402,"cache_read_input_tokens":64700,"output_tokens":4005,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6402},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4005,"cache_read_input_tokens":64700,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6402,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6402,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ae6821a60786cf8ea","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTFXWJ17ucgV9rhcawcSwM","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":63839,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":162566},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"1f3ba854-2fc9-4fec-a163-74ba3cdb505e","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DBERMBGG8emC7EBdncJ1ib","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017Fj4GUQ71aL4Bwf95f5RiC","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"provenance_weight_training","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260515T011902","overall_score":"Weak Reject","report":"# Review Report — Reviewer R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper**: Adaptive Contamination-Aware Reweighting for Pretraining on Mixed Real-Synthetic Corpora\n\n**Tier**: Median\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\nThe core technical contribution — adaptive b* via ESS-constrained maximization of alpha_eff(b) — is **limited in novelty**. I identify three layers of concern:\n\n**a) The weighting formula is not new.** The authors forthrightly acknowledge that w_i = (1-q_i)^b comes from Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025). The paper's novelty rests entirely on the *selection mechanism* for b.\n\n**b) ESS constraints are a textbook technique.** Effective sample size thresholding is standard practice in importance sampling (see Vehtari et al., JMLR 2024 on PSIS, which the authors cite in their literature map but omit from the paper). The idea of \"pick the most aggressive weighting that doesn't collapse your effective sample size below a threshold\" is not new — it is essentially the operating principle behind Pareto-smoothed importance sampling diagnostics. Applying this to a specific b parameter in a specific weighting formula constitutes an application, not a methodological advance.\n\n**c) The \"principled derivation\" framing is overstated.** The paper claims a \"theoretical framework\" but alpha_eff(b) is defined as a weighted average of (1-q_i), which is simply the normalized first moment of the weight distribution. The connection to the convergence bound d(P_t, P_0) <= t^{-min{p,alpha}} from arXiv 2602.16065 is stated as motivation but never formalized — the paper explicitly says \"motivation not contribution.\" This means the \"principled\" in \"principled b* derivation\" reduces to \"pick b to maximize a weighted mean subject to an ESS floor,\" which is a reasonable heuristic but not a theoretical contribution.\n\n**d) Advancement beyond Drayson et al. is marginal.** The paper replaces Drayson's per-model validation-based b selection with per-batch ESS-constrained selection. But the empirical evidence shows this replacement captures only ~86% of grid-best improvement, fails under cross-generator and domain-skewed settings, and the b* distribution is insensitive to model scale (identical at 0.6B and 3B). The \"zero tuning cost\" advantage is real but modest — Drayson's grid search is not computationally prohibitive for the model scales studied.\n\n**Verdict**: The novelty is below the threshold for a top venue. The contribution is an application of standard importance sampling diagnostics to a specific hyperparameter selection problem, not a new method.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Completeness\n\nThe related work section is **adequate but has notable gaps**:\n\n**Missing references:**\n- **Vehtari et al. (JMLR 2024), \"Pareto Smoothed Importance Sampling\"**: The paper uses ESS as its core constraint but does not cite the most relevant work on ESS diagnostics for importance weighting. This omission is significant because PSIS provides exactly the kind of principled importance weight management that the paper claims to introduce.\n- **Kimura & Hino (TMLR 2024), importance weighting survey**: Provides the theoretical backbone for the approach but is uncited.\n- **Alemohammad et al. (ICLR 2024), \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\"**: Relevant background on model autophagy that strengthens the motivation.\n- **Feng et al. (2024), \"Beyond Model Collapse\"**: Uses verifiers for synthetic data filtering — the hard-filtering counterpart to this paper's soft reweighting.\n- **Nait Saada et al. (2025), \"The Data-Quality Illusion\"**: Directly relevant cautionary work on classifier-based quality scores, which applies to the q-scores used here.\n\n**Positioning concerns:**\n- The paper positions itself as \"complementary\" to Drayson et al. by distinguishing recursive training vs. one-pass pretraining. But the weighting formula works identically in both settings — the distinction is about experimental setup, not methodology. A reviewer familiar with both papers would see this as the same method with a different b-selection subroutine.\n- The comparison to Golden Ratio Weighting (He et al. 2025) is fair but could note that Golden Ratio provides actual theoretical optimality guarantees, whereas adaptive b* provides a heuristic with no optimality proof.\n- The SIR vs. loss reweighting distinction is listed as a differentiator but the paper admits \"a full gradient variance analysis under both implementations remains future work.\" Without this analysis, the distinction is implementation-level, not conceptual.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Contribution Calibration\n\nThe paper's three claimed contributions need recalibration:\n\n**Contribution 1 (\"Theoretical framework\")**: Overclaimed. There is no theorem, no proof, no formal guarantee. alpha_eff(b) is a diagnostic statistic, and the ESS constraint is borrowed from importance sampling. Calling this a \"theoretical framework\" risks reviewer backlash at a top venue. More honest framing: \"a practical heuristic for automated b selection motivated by importance sampling principles.\"\n\n**Contribution 2 (\"Practical result\")**: The strongest contribution, but the headline number (85.8% of grid-best) is not compelling. At a top venue, reviewers expect methods to match or exceed baselines, not recover 86% of them. The \"zero tuning cost\" angle is legitimate but the savings are modest — 9 training runs is not prohibitive at the 200M-token scale, and the paper does not demonstrate settings where grid search is genuinely infeasible.\n\n**Contribution 3 (\"Problem setting\")**: This is not a contribution — it is a statement of the experimental conditions. \"We address the unknown contamination proportion setting\" is a problem setup, not a methodological contribution. Drayson et al. also do not assume known proportions; they use a validation set to select b, which is a different (and arguably more principled) approach to handling unknown proportions.\n\n**The \"diagnostic framework\" reframing** (visible in the worst-case paper variant) feels like a retreat. The original claim was that adaptive b* provides a principled alternative to grid search. When the method systematically overshoots by 4x and recovers only 70% of grid-best, relabeling it as a \"diagnostic tool\" does not rescue the contribution — it replaces a falsified strong claim with an unfalsifiable weak claim.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Significance\n\n**Problem importance**: High. Contaminated pretraining corpora are a genuine and growing concern. The problem is timely and practically relevant.\n\n**Solution sufficiency**: Low. The proposed method:\n- Recovers only 86% of grid-best improvement in the best case\n- Fails (performs worse than uniform) under cross-generator contamination\n- Fails under domain-skewed contamination\n- Produces b* distributions insensitive to model scale, suggesting it does not actually adapt to the problem structure\n- Shows no statistically significant downstream task improvement\n- Has a critical hyperparameter (n_min) with an extremely narrow working range (only n_min=2 works at batch_size=4)\n\nThe paper's strongest empirical finding — that TF-IDF+LR is within 0.005 PPL of ModernBERT — is genuinely useful but is an observation, not a method. It could be a paragraph in a systems paper, not the centerpiece of a methods paper.\n\n**Practitioner impact**: Low. A practitioner reading this paper would conclude: (a) use any detector with AUC > 0.9; (b) set b=0.3 and don't bother with adaptive selection; (c) the effect is 0.04 PPL at 200M tokens, which may or may not matter at scale.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Presentation\n\n**Strengths**: The paper (median variant) is clearly written. The experimental setup is well-described. Tables are clean and informative. The limitations section is unusually honest.\n\n**Weaknesses**:\n- The abstract claims \"matches grid-search-optimal performance within seed variance\" but the aggregate ratio is 85.8%, which is not \"within seed variance\" by any standard statistical definition. The per-seed ratios (95.2%, 87.0%, 78.4%) show the gap is systematic, not noise.\n- Algorithm 1 is underspecified. The paper says \"Search b in [0, b_max] for b* maximizing alpha_eff(b)\" but the actual implementation (visible in the code) uses a 1000-point grid search + bisection refinement. This is a grid search to replace a grid search — the overhead is per-batch rather than per-experiment, but the framing obscures this.\n- The paper does not report computational overhead of the per-batch b* computation. How much wall-clock time does the 1000-point grid + bisection add per training step?\n- The \"effect amplification\" sections (higher contamination, larger model) report projected numbers that appear to be from the \"dream\" simulation rather than actual experiments. The distinction between real and projected results is unclear.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Top Weaknesses\n\n**W1: The method underperforms its own baseline.** Adaptive b* recovers 85.8% of grid-best at rho=0.4, drops to 75% cross-generator, and to ~50% domain-skewed. A method paper whose main method is consistently inferior to a simple grid search over 9 values faces a fundamental viability question. The \"zero tuning cost\" argument does not overcome this when the grid search is cheap.\n\n**W2: Failure modes are not edge cases.** Cross-generator contamination (the norm in web data) and domain-skewed contamination (the norm in real corpora) are the standard conditions, not adversarial stress tests. The method works only in the easiest setting (single known generator, uniform injection) — the one setting where you'd have the most information to just set b manually.\n\n**W3: n_min sensitivity reveals fragility.** At batch_size=4, only n_min=2 works. n_min=1 leads to extremes, n_min=3 collapses to uniform. This is not a \"robust default\" — it is a knife-edge that happens to work at one batch size. The paper does not test whether n_min=2 remains optimal at batch_size=16 or 64. The internal project notes reveal that 75%+ of batches have ESS hitting the floor at 2.0, meaning the method operates at its constraint boundary almost always. This is a sign of a poorly conditioned optimization, not a well-designed algorithm.\n\n**W4: No genuine theoretical contribution.** The alpha_eff(b) \"framework\" is a weighted mean. The ESS constraint is textbook. The convergence bound is motivation, not contribution. The paper has no theorem, no proof, and no formal guarantee about b*'s behavior. The concentration bound in the code (compute_concentration_bound) is not mentioned in the paper at all.\n\n**W5: Effect size is negligible and does not transfer downstream.** 0.04 PPL improvement at 200M tokens, with no statistically significant downstream task improvement. The \"effect amplification\" argument (2x at higher contamination, 2x at larger scale) is speculative extrapolation from limited data points, and even the projected best case (1.24% relative PPL at 3B, rho=0.4) is modest.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Strengths\n\n**S1: Honest reporting.** The paper does not hide its negative results. The cross-generator degradation, domain-skewed failure, and downstream non-significance are all reported transparently. This is commendable and relatively rare.\n\n**S2: Controlled experimental design.** The use of 3 seeds, multiple contamination ratios, cross-generator testing, and domain-skewed injection represents a thorough experimental protocol. The extended grid search to b < 0.5 to verify the grid-best location shows diligence.\n\n**S3: Detector sufficiency finding.** The empirical observation that TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) is within 0.005 PPL of ModernBERT (AUC=0.986) is practically useful. If validated at scale, this would save significant compute in production pipelines.\n\n**S4: Clean comparison with Drayson et al.** The paper clearly delineates what is shared (the weighting formula) and what is new (the b-selection mechanism). The comparison table in the paper framing is well-structured.\n\n**S5: Graceful degradation.** The property that n_min >= |B| recovers uniform training (never worse than baseline) is a nice safety property, even if the effective operating range is narrow.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1**: The paper claims adaptive b* \"eliminates grid search\" but Algorithm 1 performs a 1000-point grid search over b *per batch*. Over 6100 training steps, this is 6.1M evaluations of alpha_eff and ESS, versus 9 evaluations in Drayson's approach (9 values of b, each evaluated once on a validation set). Can you clarify the computational overhead and justify the claim that this is \"zero cost\"?\n\n**Q2**: The b* distribution is nearly identical across 0.6B and 3B models (median 0.88 vs. 0.85). If b* does not adapt to model capacity, what exactly is it adapting to? Drayson et al. find that optimal b varies dramatically with model size (b=10 for small models, b=1 for 1.7B). Your method's insensitivity to model scale could be either a feature (model-agnostic) or a bug (failure to capture model-dependent dynamics). Which interpretation do you support, and what evidence distinguishes them?\n\n**Q3**: At batch_size=4, 75%+ of batches have ESS=2.0 (hitting the n_min floor). This means the adaptive algorithm is effectively just picking the largest b such that ESS=2 for most batches — a simple threshold rule, not a nuanced adaptation. Would you agree that at practical batch sizes (32, 64, 128), the method might behave very differently? If so, have you tested this?\n\n**Q4**: The cross-generator experiment shows adaptive b* achieves only 75% ratio and the domain-skewed experiment shows ~50%. In production web pretraining, contamination is always multi-generator and non-uniformly distributed. What is the practical deployment scenario where the in-distribution, uniform-injection assumption holds?\n\n**Q5**: The concentration bound function in your code (compute_concentration_bound) provides a formal error bound on alpha_eff estimation. Why is this not included in the paper? It seems like the closest thing to a genuine theoretical contribution.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Score\n\n**3 — Weak Reject**\n\nThe paper addresses a relevant problem and is honestly written, but the core methodological contribution (adaptive b* via ESS-constrained alpha_eff maximization) is an application of standard importance sampling diagnostics rather than a novel technique. The method consistently underperforms grid search, fails in realistic contamination scenarios (cross-generator, domain-skewed), and has a narrow hyperparameter working range. The strongest finding (detector sufficiency) is an empirical observation that does not require the proposed framework. The \"theoretical framework\" claim is not supported by any formal result. For a top venue at 25% acceptance, the novelty and result strength are insufficient.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Confidence\n\n**4 — High confidence.** I am familiar with importance sampling, ESS diagnostics, data reweighting for LLM pretraining, and the Drayson et al. baseline. I have read the paper, the codebase (alpha_eff.py), the internal decision log, and the project memory. My concerns about novelty and method effectiveness are grounded in both the reported results and the internal development history (e.g., n_min=3 failure requiring D012 fix, MVP passing at 88.6% under a relaxed threshold via D019)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":682,"cache_read_input_tokens":62784,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":682,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTFXWJ17ucgV9rhcawcSwM","session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"cdaec06a-8d5a-4534-8a73-b30fa3fdefbb"}
