{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTFXWJ17ucgV9rhcawcSwM","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)** | **Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nThe review identifies five primary weaknesses:\n\n1. **Method underperforms its own baseline** -- adaptive b* recovers only 85.8% of grid-best, dropping to 75% cross-generator and ~50% domain-skewed.\n\n2. **Failure modes are the standard conditions** -- cross-generator and domain-skewed contamination are the norm in web data, not edge cases. The method works only in the easiest (single known generator, uniform injection) setting.\n\n3. **n_min sensitivity reveals fragility** -- only n_min=2 works at batch_size=4, with 75%+ of batches hitting the ESS floor, indicating the algorithm operates at its constraint boundary almost always.\n\n4. **No genuine theoretical contribution** -- alpha_eff(b) is a weighted mean, the ESS constraint is textbook importance sampling, and the convergence bound is acknowledged as motivation only. The concentration bound in the code is not even mentioned in the paper.\n\n5. **Negligible effect size with no downstream transfer** -- 0.04 PPL improvement, no statistically significant downstream gains.\n\nThe review acknowledges strengths in honest reporting, thorough experimental design, the detector sufficiency finding, and clean positioning vs. Drayson et al. The core novelty concern is that ESS-constrained b selection is an application of standard importance sampling diagnostics to a specific problem, not a new technique. The report was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/median/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae6821a60786cf8ea (use SendMessage with to: 'ae6821a60786cf8ea' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 67979\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 178697</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5659f699-0e1d-4903-a3c6-9903938af801","uuid":"c38cad0b-2433-4972-abd8-2725f802a134","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:24:23.079Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a blind peer review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Adaptive Provenance Weighting for Contaminated Pretraining Corpora\" proposes adaptive b*, an online method that derives the weighting exponent b in w_i = (1-q_i)^b from per-batch contamination statistics α̂_eff(b) subject to an ESS constraint. Three claimed contributions:\n1. Lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector suffices (within 0.003 PPL of ModernBERT 150M)\n2. α̂_eff(b) framework as diagnostic tool (b* overshoots grid-optimal by ~4×, but trajectory reveals contamination structure)\n3. Adaptive b* as zero-cost reasonable default (~70% of grid-best), fails under cross-gen and domain-skew\n\nAdditional claimed contribution: loss reweighting vs SIR comparison.\n\n## Method Details\n- α̂_eff(b) = 1 - (1/n)Σ(1-q_i)^b — effective contamination fraction under weighting exponent b\n- ESS(B,b) = (Σ(1-q_i)^b)² / Σ(1-q_i)^{2b} — effective sample size\n- b* = max{b≥0 : ESS(B,b) ≥ n_min} — largest b satisfying ESS constraint\n- Loss reweighting: normalize weights to mean 1, apply as multipliers to per-sample loss\n- Algorithm 1: grid search over b in {0, 0.1, ..., b_max}, pick largest satisfying ESS≥n_min\n\n## Related Work Cited\n- Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025): detect-and-reweight via SIR, the direct predecessor\n- Shumailov et al. (Nature 2024): model collapse\n- Dohmatob et al. (ICLR 2025): strong model collapse\n- Xie et al. (NeurIPS 2023): DSIR — data selection via importance resampling\n- Xie et al. (NeurIPS 2023): DoReMi — domain reweighting\n- Abbas et al. (ACL 2024): SoftDedup\n- Li et al. (arXiv 2025): golden ratio weighting (w*=1/φ, assumes known k)\n- Anonymous (arXiv 2025): convergence bounds (motivates α_eff)\n- Okanovic et al. (ICLR 2025): dynamic loss-based reweighting\n- Hoffmann et al. (NeurIPS 2022): Chinchilla scaling laws\n\n## Key Results That Bear on Novelty Assessment\n- Adaptive b* overshoots optimal by 4× (median 0.87 vs grid-best 0.2)\n- Only recovers 70% of grid-best improvement\n- Fails in cross-generator scenario (adaptive LOSES to uniform)\n- Fails in domain-skewed scenario (adaptive LOSES to uniform)\n- n_min has narrow sweet spot (only n_min=2 works at batch_size=4)\n- 3B model: b* distribution identical to 0.6B (method doesn't adapt to model)\n- No downstream task improvement detectable\n- The \"strongest\" finding is arguably detector sufficiency (TF-IDF≈ModernBERT), which is more of an empirical observation than a methodological contribution\n- Loss reweighting vs SIR comparison is listed as contribution #3 but the paper says \"a full gradient variance analysis under both implementations remains future work\" — the comparison is largely conceptual\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** — How novel is adaptive b*? Is ESS-constrained b* selection genuinely new, or is it a standard technique applied to a specific problem? How much does the method advance beyond Drayson et al.?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness** — Are there important missing references? Is the positioning relative to prior work accurate and fair?\n\n3. **Contribution Calibration** — Are the three claimed contributions appropriately scoped? Is there overclaiming or underclaiming? Does the \"diagnostic framework\" framing feel like a retreat from the original stronger claim?\n\n4. **Significance** — Would this paper influence how practitioners handle contaminated pretraining data? Is the problem important enough and the solution sufficient?\n\n5. **Presentation** — Is the paper well-written? Are the claims clearly stated? Is the narrative coherent given the largely negative results?\n\n6. **Weaknesses** — Top 3-5 weaknesses with evidence\n\n7. **Strengths** — What does the paper do well?\n\n8. **Questions for Authors** — 3-5 specific questions\n\n9. **Score** — 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n10. **Confidence** — 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: the paper's main method (adaptive b*) demonstrably underperforms a simple grid search by 30%, fails in two realistic scenarios, and doesn't adapt to model scale. Evaluate whether the reframing as \"diagnostic framework\" and the empirical findings (detector sufficiency) are enough to carry the paper.\n","agentId":"ae6821a60786cf8ea","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)** | **Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\nThe review identifies five primary weaknesses:\n\n1. **Method underperforms its own baseline** -- adaptive b* recovers only 85.8% of grid-best, dropping to 75% cross-generator and ~50% domain-skewed.\n\n2. **Failure modes are the standard conditions** -- cross-generator and domain-skewed contamination are the norm in web data, not edge cases. The method works only in the easiest (single known generator, uniform injection) setting.\n\n3. **n_min sensitivity reveals fragility** -- only n_min=2 works at batch_size=4, with 75%+ of batches hitting the ESS floor, indicating the algorithm operates at its constraint boundary almost always.\n\n4. **No genuine theoretical contribution** -- alpha_eff(b) is a weighted mean, the ESS constraint is textbook importance sampling, and the convergence bound is acknowledged as motivation only. The concentration bound in the code is not even mentioned in the paper.\n\n5. **Negligible effect size with no downstream transfer** -- 0.04 PPL improvement, no statistically significant downstream gains.\n\nThe review acknowledges strengths in honest reporting, thorough experimental design, the detector sufficiency finding, and clean positioning vs. Drayson et al. The core novelty concern is that ESS-constrained b selection is an application of standard importance sampling diagnostics to a specific problem, not a new technique. The report was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/median/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":178697,"totalTokens":67979,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4099,"cache_read_input_tokens":63466,"output_tokens":413,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4099},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":413,"cache_read_input_tokens":63466,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4099,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4099,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
